blob
mark :1
original-oid b9d3abd0ca72c376006631924d7ac8c8f15e938b
data 124
Article One: Purpose of this Law
This Law governs the evidence and its production.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: GENERAL PROVISIONS

blob
mark :2
original-oid baa9f53598365a7c2c6ab6ec7ab43f803773ae77
data 5826
Article 2: Interpretation
In this Law:
(a) "electronic data" means electronic representations of information in any form;
(b) "electronic certificate" means a record which is issued by an electronic certification service provider for the purpose of confirming the identity or other significant characteristics of the person who holds a particular pair of keys;
(c) "evidence" means a means of demonstrating the truth of a fact;
(d) "expert evidence" means testimony intended to give explanations and a conclusion based on expertise that goes beyond the ordinary knowledge of a judge or a person ruling on a dispute within the scope of his or her duties, due to the special expertise of the subject of the dispute;
(e) "presumption" means inferences that the law or a competent organ makes from a known fact to demonstrate an unknown fact;
(f) "legal presumption" means presumption that is provided by the law thereby allowing the competent organ to confirm something without requiring an evidence unless the contrary is established;
(g) "human presumption" means presumption that is established by the discretion and wisdom of a person ruling on a dispute;
(h) "material evidence" means a piece of evidence brought to the knowledge of the competent organ by showing the status of a certain object, a trace of evidence or the status of the very person;
(i) "testimonial evidence" means:
(i) an oral or written statement an individual makes before a competent organ regarding what he or she personally saw or heard, that is relevant to the matter in dispute;
(ii) any information provided to the competent organ with reference to a fact which could be perceived by any sense other than the ones provided under Sub- subparagraph (i) or in any other manner, with reference to a fact which could be perceived by any other sense or manner;
(iii) an opinion that is provided to the competent organ by the holder with reference to the dispute under consideration;
(j) "scientific evidence" means information gathered from scientific and technological research or any of them to support or contradict a claim;
(k) "electronic evidence" means electronic data of probative value to an investigation that is stored in, received or transmitted through an electronic device;
(l) "commencement of proof in writing" means a document emanating from the person against whom the evidence is submitted or his or her representative and which makes the alleged fact plausible;
(m) "document" means a set of alphabetic signs or other intelligible signs affixed to a medium whether electronic or not, enabling it to be accessed, whatever the nature of the medium and the methods of its transmission;
(n) "private deed" means a document that bears a fingerprint or a signature of one or several private parties or of their representatives, or an electronic record whose content is binding only on the parties deed;
(o) "authentication deed" means a document on which a person affixes his or her fingerprint or signature, after swearing in before a person authorized by law and which confirms that the content of the document is true;
(p) "electronic record" means the electronic data, including documents, sounds, videos; produced, generated, recorded, processed, stored, sent, communicated or received using a computer or any other electronic device, that can be read or perceived by a person by means of a computer system or other electronic device including a display, print-out or other output of those electronic data;
(q) "secure electronic record" means an electronic record to which any security procedure has been applied from a specific point of time to the time of verification;
(r) "Authentic deed" means a document written or received following a certain set of formalities by a person upon whom the law confers the power to draw it within the jurisdiction in which it is made;
(s) "domestic document" means a document, written by a person and not intended for another person, which, regardless of the place where it is written, can be used as evidence;
(t) "authentication" means the process by which a knowledge or fact-based evidence is proven to correspond to what it claims to be;
(u) "Minister" means the Minister in charge of justice;
(v) "electronic records system" means the computer system or any other electronic device by or in which electronic data is recorded or stored;
(w) "admission" means an oral or written statement a party or his or her representative makes to accept something;
(x) "signature" means a sign or a set of signs of a person drawn by hand, electronically or by other means that distinguish its owner from other persons;
(y) "electronic signature" means information placed on an electronic record, which helps to logically associate an electronic record with its author or to confirm the information contained in this record;
(z) "secure electronic signature" means an electronic signature that is secure and has the following characteristics:
(i) being unique to the person using it;
(ii) being capable of identifying its author;
(iii) being created in a manner or using a means under the sole control of its author;
(iv) being linked to the electronic record to which it relates and such a record did not change;
(aa) "digital signature" means an electronic signature consisting of a transformation of an electronic record using an asymmetric cryptosystem;
(bb) "person devoid of the capacity to be a witness" means a person with disability, age or physical appearance that refrain him or her from narrating facts in his or her depositions or the person who is stripped of the right to be a witness by the court;
(cc) "competent organ" means a court or an organ which receives a claim that falls within its competence or which decides on it, basing on evidence.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: GENERAL PROVISIONS

blob
mark :3
original-oid 100e3cc8198fb135a591808b0732b831a0e98e7b
data 256
Article 3: Purpose of this Law
This Law relates to evidence produced before the court. It can also apply to other institutions with powers to settle disputes without prejudice to evidence provided by specific laws.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: GENERAL PROVISIONS

blob
mark :4
original-oid c3c3f3aebd2d73226e54a10d13fe03880e8d6a54
data 635
Article 4: Burden of proof
(1) A claimant has the burden to prove his or her claim. The respondent provides arguments substantiating his or her defence. However, a judge or an adjudicator may order the party to a claim or any other person to produce evidence he or she possesses.
(2) The decision of the competent organ which orders to produce or demonstrate an evidence indicates the identity of a party to the claim or any other person who must produce or demonstrate such evidence, as well as the mode and time period for so doing. This decision is notified to any person concerned therewith.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: COMMON PROVISIONS

blob
mark :5
original-oid c4c7a7e162f12ea292031573c5de2a811aeb477c
data 1173
Article 5: Effects of refusing to produce evidence
(1) Following the decision by the competent organ, a party to the claim or one of the contending parties who refuses, without valid reasons, to produce an evidence may lose the case as consequence.
(2) In case the person who refused to produce an evidence is a third party, he or she is summoned before the competent organ to justify such a refusal, unless the law provides otherwise. A Judge or any other adjudicator may sanction him or her to the payment of late penalties which may be calculated on a daily, weekly or monthly basis in order to compel him or her to produce the evidence.
(3) Where the sanction applies to a physical person, the amount of late penalties referred to in Paragraph (2) of this Article cannot go beyond FRW 5,000 daily, FRW 50,000 weekly and FRW 300,000 monthly. When the person refusing to produce evidence is a public institution, a company, a cooperative, a partnership, a Trust, a Foundation or any other legal entity irrespective of their nature, the late penalties cannot go beyond FRW 50,000 daily, FRW 200,000 weekly and FRW 1,000,000 monthly.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: COMMON PROVISIONS

blob
mark :6
original-oid cbde7ff2d94d0bf7c4cbdf3daa1fc2b164df0ba0
data 206
Article 6: Applicable types of evidence
Any competent organ decides a claim before it in accordance with the types of evidence applicable to the nature of that claim.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: COMMON PROVISIONS

blob
mark :7
original-oid d13edcedd55e9009d795aac5eba2d6bb6c14527e
data 211
Article 7: Prohibited ways of collecting evidence
Collecting evidence through torture, degrading acts, fraud or any other means that are contrary to the law is prohibited.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: COMMON PROVISIONS

blob
mark :8
original-oid e3d7c71c802c20491fe5a5c5f6e825d680855688
data 161
Article 8: Presenting evidence by an authentication deed
Evidence may be presented in the form of an authentication deed.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: COMMON PROVISIONS

blob
mark :9
original-oid fbaa4127020d346a76ec5d2384ccce673939aa99
data 393
Article 9: Components of the documentary evidence
Documentary evidence consists of a set of letters, characters, numbers or other signs or symbols endowed with an intelligible meaning, regardless of their medium and the way they are transmitted.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :10
original-oid 3d7487f8750a48b8027dc7b167855f512b548434
data 1121
Article 10: Probative value of the authentic deed
(1) The authentic deed witnessed by a competent person in the capacity of a witness or agent within the limit of his or her mission is reliable and binding towards concerned parties. Provisions of the authentic deed cannot be challenged except through prosecution for forgery or falsification.
(2) An authentic deed is reliable for other contents, enunciations and sincerity of parties if it is not challenged through any other documentary evidence or by any evidence supported by a commencement of proof in writing, if it is refuted by a person who affixed his or her fingerprint or signature on it, or in case it is attacked by a third party, the later may challenge it by any other legally accepted evidence.
(3) A deed that appears to be authentic because it was drawn by an incompetent person or if it was drawn through unlawful means is treated as a private deed if it bears the fingerprints or signatures of parties.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :11
original-oid b064c3f89390881f3432c0329a43dd5fbeabd557
data 643
Article 11: Probative value of a private deed
A private deed admitted by the person against whom it is invoked or which is otherwise legally admitted is reliable until proven otherwise in writing or supported by a commencement of proof in writing if it is refuted by a party to it, or until it is challenged through any other legal means if it is refuted by a third party to the deed, except for the provisions regarding opposability of the date to third parties who are not parties to the deed.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :12
original-oid 2fe8ff1231918c167b849e8ea76dad5de3d0808f
data 812
Article 12: Exact date of a private deed with regard to third parties
A private deed has no exact date with respect to third parties until antedate has become impossible. This is so in particular:
(a) if the private deed is officially registered, the date considered definite is that of registration;
(b) if the provisions of the deed are proved by an authentic deed, the document is taken to have been made on the date of authentication;
(c) if the party or one of the signatories died or faces permanent disability making him or her incapable to make an act, the definite date of signature is considered to be that of occurrence of death or permanent disability.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :13
original-oid a8a19e105ffc516bafd99a59cb2aae9811fa3f97
data 971
Article 13: Validity of a synallagmatic private deed
(1) A private synallagmatic deed is only valid if it bears a fingerprint or if it is signed or accepted by all parties to the agreement, their heirs or their proxies.
(2) The parties with different interests may make as many original documents of the agreement as their number.
(3) The original document of the agreement states the number of the original copies made and must be complete to the extent of not adding other notes, numbers or anything else and bear the fingerprint or signature of all parties to the contract.
(4) The omission of formality to mention the number of the original copies made does not invalidate the original document that bears the fingerprint or signature and may not be opposed by a party who has executed his or her part of the agreement.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :14
original-oid e1ca63f054c3deb52ca0992eb2c499bd8773b0ed
data 554
Article 14: Binding effect of a deed
An authentic deed or a private deed is considered binding between the contracting parties even on such matters expressed only in the form of a statement as long as the statement has direct relevance to the agreement. The statement which has no direct relevance to the agreement is considered as the commencement of evidence in writing only for parties to the agreement.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :15
original-oid 0c0c4cb46eddefcdc5b3aace319d592192c56b1d
data 827
Article 15: Promissory note or private promise
(1) A promissory note or private promise by which one party commits himself or herself to another to pay a sum of money or a substantial consideration must bear the fingerprint or signature of the author as well as the amount or the quantity of that consideration in words and numbers.
(2) However, a unilateral agreement bearing a fingerprint or signed by the concerned party may be validly summarised by the note "valid or approved for" followed by the sum or value of the consideration in numbers followed by the same amount written in words between brackets. In case of contradiction, the sum or value written in words prevails.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :16
original-oid e390cc8727b43b86933f7dd59e163a92fac5bd74
data 539
Article 16: Inconsistency in the figure expressed in a private deed
In case the figure mentioned in a private deed differs from that mentioned in "valid or approved for" note, the obligation is presumed to be concerned with the lesser figure, even if the private deed and the note were handwritten in words by the party committing himself or herself, unless the basis of the error is proved.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :17
original-oid 91f5f80e4413d2abb0d24443ba449d3b2ff162bb
data 605
Article 17: Probative value of traders' documents between traders and non- traders
(1) A trader's document is not binding on people not engaged in commerce as proof of goods supplied. However, a trader's document may be admitted as evidence against the trader him or herself.
(2) A person seeking to rely on a trader's document as evidence may not reject the contents of the document contrary to his or her allegations where the document is duly registered.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :18
original-oid 704ef59753a1f8634063f7d57bdd28e634bbfdc0
data 380
Article 18: Probative value of traders' documents between traders themselves
In cases or issues related to business, the business document may be accepted as an evidence by the competent organ between the businesspersons themselves.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :19
original-oid 343d1e1f06383aa6b31593ca7bd8211599eef12e
data 374
Article 19: Request for production of traders' documents
The competent organ may, during hearing of a claim, order the presentation of trader's documents, even ex officio, for any useful purpose in the assessment of the claim.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

