mark :20
original-oid 98a8e54a75c75e865b8d07f3729fdfa1c595dba0
data 365
Article 20: Probative value of domestic document
A domestic document written by a party does not constitute an evidence towards him or her. However, a domestic document written by a party is invoked against the party.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :21
original-oid f3d0888813d195801546933d3ac8503cc912156c
data 469
Article 21: Probative value of annotations to the deed
An annotation made by a creditor to the deed with which he or she remained indicating the debtor's discharge of liability constitutes evidence of a debtor's discharge from liability even if it does not bear the fingerprint or signature of the creditor, and the date.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :22
original-oid 5f1873bb0391fd68d8f5c7d4667fbbfc936f7083
data 512
Article 22: Probative value of a document sent by a person to another
A document that a person sends to another is used as evidence against its author, even if it bears no fingerprint or a signature of his or her, as long as it may be established that it emanated from the authoring party. The document constitutes an admission made outside of the competent organ.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
