mark :23
original-oid 3c6c5a0daf1035efbe4508491094a20d1d7eacbc
data 304
Article 23: Probative value of counter- letters
A counter-letter produces effects only towards contracting parties; it is ineffective against third parties.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :24
original-oid b657dd885fc5d5a3895e1384b4b295749cdaf4fd
data 638
Article 24: Probative value of a recognitive act
A recognitive act does not preclude the presentation of the primordial act unless its content particularly is spelt in the document. The content of a recognitive act not contained in the primordial act or which is contrary to it is of no effect. However, if there are several similar recognitive acts in possession of the creditor, and one of them is dated 30 years back, the creditor may be relieved from presentation of the primordial act.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :25
original-oid ee5c909882071b9d0e7e46a3b7041d379639b065
data 1052
Article 25: Act of confirmation or ratification
(1) The act of confirmation or ratification against which the law admits the action of nullity or for rescission by the competent authority is valid only when it indicates –
(a) the substance of that obligation;
(b) grounds of the action of rescission; and
(c) the intention to rectify the defect on which that action is founded.
(2) In default of an act of confirmation or ratification, it is sufficient that the obligation be voluntarily executed after the expiration of the period the obligation could have been confirmed or ratified.
(3) Confirmation, ratification or voluntary execution of obligations in accordance with provisions of the law is equivalent to renunciation of facts and defence that might be submitted during the argument relating to the act but which are not prejudicial to the rights of persons who are not parties to the contract.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :26
original-oid 21f4701c4caca526a8f06eb6c933a0cb230783bb
data 290
Article 26: Probative value of commencement of proof in writing
Commencement of proof in writing is valid only if supported by other evidence.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :27
original-oid 1ec94096e8a6e7cd3ea62cd2d49cb75ea7fd0d3a
data 674
Article 27: Probative value of copies of an original document
(1) A copy of an original document possessed by a legally authorised person is valid to all without necessarily proving the authority, the fingerprint or signature of that person.
(2) A certified copy or any other certified reproduction of the original document is always a reliable proof until proven otherwise.
(3) A copy which is not certified true copy of the original is valid only if supported by any other evidence when the original copy cannot be produced.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Authentic and private deeds

blob
mark :28
original-oid abaf9fb8b23a8feebcac05478557d0b70418a71f
data 528
Article 28: Denial of a private deed
(1) A party against whom a private deed is introduced may deny that it is not him or her or his or her representative that handwrote, fingerprinted or signed it.
(2) An heir or legatee is allowed to declare that the testator, legator or their respective representative is not the author of the handwriting, fingerprint or signature.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Production and verification of documents

blob
mark :29
original-oid bfa1521da2abbd2b0c76f406c1f46b3af2e2787c
data 613
Article 29: Disregarding the denial of handwriting, fingerprint or signature
If a party contests the handwriting, fingerprint or signature attributed to him or her, or claims ignorance of the handwriting attributed to its author, the competent organ may disregard it and takes a decision if it has evidence that the plea is purely dilatory or irrelevant to the taking of decision on the dispute and if no request for verification of the writing was made.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Production and verification of documents

blob
mark :30
original-oid 5f8801016f6b9c32856d7ab9614f4b96ba67b208
data 845
Article 30: Document verification procedure
(1) In case of denial of a deed, the competent organ, upon request by one of the parties or on its own initiative in the interest of justice orders the verification of such a deed.
(2) The competent organ seizes any disavowed or contested document, which is subjected to a minute indicating its major features and content and bearing the fingerprint or signature of the judge or any other adjudicator who also invites the parties to affix their fingerprints or signatures thereon.
(3) The competent organ appoints an expert and orders the parties to produce the documents required for comparison and which it provides, in turn, to the expert.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Production and verification of documents

blob
mark :31
original-oid ebba680fdc76db1f00e3949372a2143e24fdbc05
data 684
Article 31: Submission of document by third party for comparison
If the document required for comparison is in the hands of a third party, the competent organ orders that party to submit it to the organ which produces a copy and minutes thereof. Such a copy indicates that the original document is kept by the competent organ and bears the names of the adjudicator and his or her fingerprint or signature. This copy is kept by the custodian of the original copy in order to replace the original document until it is returned.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Production and verification of documents

blob
mark :32
original-oid ef1cee2e16e03f18b0e5747da84b2db13c485f96
data 982
Article 32: Applicable procedure in case of lack or insufficiency of documents for comparison
(1) In case of absence or insufficiency of documents for comparison, a judge or an adjudicator orders that the person presumed to be the author of the document or the person whose fingerprint or signature is appended thereon, writes a similar and appends his or her fingerprint or signature on the document for comparison in the presence of the expert.
(2) If the presumed author of the document, who affixed a fingerprint or a signature on it refuses to write the dictated text, or refuses to put his or her fingerprint or signature on the document for comparison, the document he or she is alleged to have written may be admitted against that person as the one who wrote it and affixed his or he fingerprint or signature on it.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Production and verification of documents

blob
mark :33
original-oid 26026d728c80496b946e565e466ae1b900b6cf4e
data 1133
Article 33: Verification of the disavowed or contested document, fingerprint or signature
(1) If formalities referred to from Article 28 to 31 of this Law are completed, the competent organ orders that the expert verifies the disavowed or contested document, fingerprint or signature.
(2) the expert is given at least –
(i) the decision of the competent organ attesting the claim for the verification of the disavowed or contested document, fingerprint or signature;
(ii) the disavowed or contested document, fingerprint or signature and a statement indicating their status at the time they were submitted to the competent organ;
(iii) the document, fingerprint or signature for comparison if any;
(iv) the statements of witnesses in support of their depositions, if need be.
(3) After being sworn in, expert proceeds and examines the document, fingerprint or signature and if there are more than one expert they jointly carry out such an examination and produce a report.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section One: Documentary evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Production and verification of documents

