mark :34
original-oid 6dd359ca64fe12d6ca8dbe0425f6efd690c22b8b
data 525
Article 34: Admissibility and probative value of scientific and digital evidence
(1) Evidence collected through scientific and digital means or either of them is admissible and has the same probative value as other evidence if an expert confirms its truthfulness.
(2) An Order of the Minister may determine a specific procedure for the collection and presentation of some scientific and digital evidence or either evidence.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :35
original-oid d48799809e79f2ed8323471955053aa59b84563e
data 269
Article 35: Admissibility and probative value of an electronic record
An electronic record has admissibility and probative value in case it has been lawfully obtained.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :36
original-oid 1c146557b91de363e37fedb7790d35fe71d67934
data 579
Article 36: Principle to produce the original of electronic record
(1) An original of electronic record is required in order to prove its content, unless otherwise provided.
(2) The principle to produce the original of electronic record is respected if –
(a) the integrity of the electronic record system and of the manner in which the electronic record was produced proves that is has not been changed; and
(b) the integrity of an electronic record is proven or undoubtable.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :37
original-oid 3b92cf1907aff987ec6b3a1ab43ae2747c8b59a6
data 452
Article 37: Admissibility of an electronic record copy
Without prejudice to provisions of Article 35 of this Law, an electronic record copy is considered as an original electronic record if–
(a) the original has been damaged, lost or cannot be found; or
(b) has already been admissible and used as an electronic record produced or stored as a copy.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :38
original-oid b6e5d30ffb6f2347f248fadfa0cb522cff794f9b
data 1465
Article 38: Presumption of integrity of electronic record
The competent organ may presume the integrity of an electronic record if –
(a) it remained complete and unaltered except the addition of any endorsement or any immaterial change, which arises in the normal course of communication, storage or display;
(b) it was certified or has been electronically signed by use of the method specified by an accredited certification entity;
(c) the integrity and content of the electronic record were notarized;
(d) it was recorded in a non-rewritable electronic record system or any other electronic means that does not allow alteration of electronic record;
(e) it was examined and its integrity confirmed by an expert;
(f) there was evidence the results obtained at a time when the computer system or other electronic device was functioning properly, or its malfunction did not compromise the integrity of the electronic records;
(g) it is established that the electronic record tendered in evidence by one of the parties was recorded or stored by a party to the dispute against whom it is tendered in evidence; or
(h) it is established that the electronic record was recorded or stored in the usual and ordinary course of business by a person who is not a party and who did not record or store it under the control of the party seeking to introduce it as evidence.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :39
original-oid 0004b1d0591c8668472cf083e35e990d4790e34c
data 458
Article 39: Admissibility of electronic data provided as hearsay
A statement contained in an electronic record produced by a computer or by another electronic device, which constitutes hearsay, is inadmissible as evidence of any fact stated therein unless the integrity of the computer or of another electronic device or of the electronic record is proven.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :40
original-oid 921e34569da843fae62eefc50860488dc1433003
data 323
Article 40: Agreement on electronic evidence
Subject to the discretion of the competent organ, electronic evidence is admissible if the parties have expressly agreed that at any time its admissibility may not be disputed.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :41
original-oid f3cd4e8edbda0526e2576a038614a7abb7fc9b9a
data 391
Article 41: Admissibility of an electronic record from another country
An electronic record from another country is admissible if its integrity or the integrity of the computer or of another electronic device by which it was recorded or in which it was stored is proven or may be presumed.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :42
original-oid b56c1d4765a749f07c5c52fc7b303b17275176dc
data 284
Article 42: Admissibility and probative value of an electronic signature
An electronic signature is admissible and has probative value as any other evidence in any legal proceedings.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :43
original-oid 55c285ef57339f90bd6fa5b20839c55f6e7486e1
data 318
Article 43: Production of electronic signature as evidence
A person who claims that an electronic signature is of a person to whom he or she attributes it must prove it, except if it is a secure electronic signature.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :44
original-oid 47e2365a5f4c9944fd4d0649048ef03fa3ad9010
data 828
Article 44: Presumption relating to secure electronic record and signature
(1) In case of a dispute on secure electronic record, unless there is evidence to the contrary, it shall be presumed that the secure electronic record has not been modified since it was produced.
(2) In case of a dispute on secure electronic signature, unless there is evidence to the contrary, it is presumed that the secure electronic signature –
(a) is the signature of the person to whom it correlates; or
(b) was affixed to a document by the person to whom it is attributed with the intention to sign or approve the document.
(3) When an electronic record or an electronic signature is not secure, the presumption does not have probative force.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :45
original-oid 323415a2ae293ed529e83e43115362b5ea732fc1
data 836
Article 45: Electronic certificate or signature issued abroad
(1) An electronic certificate or signature issued outside the country has the same legal effect as that issued in the country if it meets the legal requirements.
(2) In determining whether an electronic certificate or signature offers a substantially equivalent level of reliability, regard is given to international standards.
(3) If concerned parties agree on the use of certain types of electronic signatures or certificates, that agreement is recognized as sufficient for the purposes of recognition of foreign electronic signature or certificate if the agreement is not contrary to domestic law of where the electronic signature or certificate is intended to be used.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :46
original-oid 94ec15d96830dcee2774c9a20d4d55a7e6def842
data 279
Article 46: Admissibility and probative value of a digital signature
A digital signature is admissible as evidence and is equivalent to the signature of a person on a hard copy.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :47
original-oid 692fbd44a49473aa0b8b1e2a417951e69cd90afc
data 529
Article 47: Verification of a digital signature
When verifying a digital signature to determine whether it is that of the person claiming to have affixed it, the competent organ may order –
(a) that person or the certification service provider to produce the certificate; or
(b) application of the procedure indicated on the electronic certificate and verification of the digital signature purportedly affixed by that person.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :48
original-oid f663cc8719008e28b868e6440f81f14143d9b4e1
data 571
Article 48: Presumption relating to a digital signature
Upon authentication of a digital signature, it is presumption that –
(a) the information contained in its electronic certificate is correct;
(b) it was created during the operational period of its electronic certificate;
(c) the message associated with a digital signature has not been altered from the time it was signed; or
(d) the certificate has been issued by the certification authority indicated therein.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :49
original-oid be9426b9033ae1a85e810f6bc1cd09961a8b9619
data 336
Article 49: Presumption of the electronic certificate
The competent authority presumes that the information indicated in the electronic certificate is correct if the certificate was accepted by the subscriber, unless proved otherwise.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 2: Scientific and digital evidences

blob
mark :50
original-oid 2d2d3fca9448f3cdc375b479281ca308b25c1784
data 614
Article 50: Procedure for giving a testimony
(1) A person gives testimony before the competent organ or not before the competent organ by which the witness is not seen by person in a manner that is visible or not visible to persons not concerned with the trial or the issue under consideration.
(2) A testimony is submitted through–
(a) oral way;
(b) writings;
(c) signs; or
(d) video, recording or any other technological means.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Procedure for giving a testimony and persons eligible to testify

blob
mark :51
original-oid 5cffc5eb9558925759debae955a16fc36df92b5e
data 703
Article 51: Person capable of giving testimony
(1) Any person may give testimony before the competent organ except a person devoid of the capacity to be a witness.
(2) A minor under the age of 14 years as well as an incapable adult who is able to narrate facts are allowed to testify before the competent organ without taking oath, but other evidence must corroborate his or her testimony.
(3) A person who is unable to speak may give his or her testimony in any other manner in which he or she may make it intelligible.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Procedure for giving a testimony and persons eligible to testify

blob
mark :52
original-oid aa9afffb15679c01fe9088f4676d41dbca01ed6c
data 878
Article 52: Cases in which testimonial evidence is not admitted
(1) Subject to special legal provisions, testimonial evidence is not admitted in relation to an unwritten contract of an amount exceeding FRW 50,000 or any object whose value exceeds such amount of money, unless other evidences support the testimony.
(2) The testimonial evidence may not be admitted if it states or does not state the content of a document, or where it is alleged to have preceded or followed the establishment of the document even if it is declared for the amount of money less than FRW 50,000 or any object whose value is less than such amount of money.
(3) Testimonial evidence may prove commercial obligations.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Procedure for giving a testimony and persons eligible to testify

blob
mark :53
original-oid cac13f5c78dc3cc4b5eb670f6010e448ea9604cf
data 498
Article 53: Admissibility and probative value of testimonial evidence
The competent organ assesses the admissibility or inadmissibility and probative value of testimonial evidence. It is not bound by the number of witnesses. Instead, it mainly considers their knowledge of facts and sincerity of their declarations.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Procedure for giving a testimony and persons eligible to testify

blob
mark :54
original-oid 1c15cbf0c74c864d7cd5f8f345a0f61fe8b46d43
data 617
Article 54: Request for using a witness
A party who seeks to use a witness makes such a request to the competent authority, giving reasons for the request in a precise and succinct manner. After assessment, the competent organ may order the use of a witness. However, the competent organ may also order, on its own motion, the deposition of testimonial evidence for facts that should be conclusive, if it is not prohibited by the law.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection One: Procedure for giving a testimony and persons eligible to testify

blob
mark :55
original-oid ec12d1ffa9dae28846d50fc48d8113e1908ee578
data 621
Article 55: Information required before testifying
A witness, before testifying, is required to state his or her full identification. He or she is also required to provide information on the following and the competent organ records it:
(a) Any relation of kinship or marriage, and the degree to which it exists, with one of the parties;
(b) his or her relationship of subordination with any of the parties, if any;
(c) any other ground likely to make his or her testimony prejudicial.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Witness examination

blob
mark :56
original-oid ff3b945abbd7af2a0e9acc985dd33771bd86b5ce
data 357
Article 56: Witness oath
Before testifying, a witness who is required to take oath, swears to tell the truth in the following words: "I………., swear to tell the truth. May I face the rigors of the law if I tell lies".
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Witness examination

blob
mark :57
original-oid d443a6786f761cce20c9b0ba106aef85080b4661
data 545
Article 57: Procedure for examination of a witness
The competent organ examines a witness separately in the absence of other witnesses, but in the presence of parties or if necessary, in their absence. It may also, on its own motion or upon request by the parties, re- examine witnesses or accept cross- examination between witnesses or between witnesses and parties, unless otherwise provided for by the law.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Witness examination

blob
mark :58
original-oid d374736ec60a7f682d2ff8a86977265f8f994714
data 713
Article 58: Procedure of testimony deposition
(1) A witness is not allowed to read from his or her notes while testifying. He or she is also not authorized to know declarations of other witnesses except when they are summoned for cross- examination between witnesses or between witnesses and parties.
(2) The competent organ records the declaration of a witness and subsequently reads it to him or her. The witness signs the declaration or affixes a fingerprint. If the witness refuses to either sign or affix a fingerprint, the competent organ records the reasons for refusal.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Witness examination

blob
mark :59
original-oid 269a910ac9449f5a4cb4c40b6d844f677c19cc8a
data 686
Article 59: Examination of witness with reliable information
Any witness with reliable information for the resolution of a claim is heard. Neither the competent organ is allowed to prevent him or her from giving testimony, nor shall the parties disqualify him or her. However, the parties have the right to state all their grounds of doubt on partiality vis-a-vis a witness and the competent organ records them. The competent organ has exclusive powers to examine the basis of such grounds and decide on the probative value that they seem to deserve.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Witness examination

blob
mark :60
original-oid 20ce1ad8c64e8c555cab9cac238cde83129bfc6f
data 616
Article 60: Obligation to appear and testify
(1) A person duly summoned as a witness must appear before the competent organ and testify. However, a person who gets confidential information by virtue of his or her status or profession may be exempted from testifying unless it can be established that the facts came to his or her knowledge outside professional secrecy.
(2) A party's spouse, even if divorced, or his or her relative up to the second degree, may decline to testify.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 3: Testimonial evidence
Subsection: Subsection 2: Witness examination

blob
mark :61
original-oid 3bbd7cc027eb0ff6d08fdf913ef399db0abd02b7
data 788
Article 61: Appointment of expert
(1) If a competent organ considers that the expert evidence is necessary in examination of a claim, it appoints an expert to carry out that examination.
However, if parties agree on one expert to be appointed, the competent organ accepts their choice.
(2) In case three experts are required, the competent organ may allow each party to appoint one expert within the time limit set by the organ, while the third expert is appointed by the competent organ.
(3) The decision of the competent organ to use expert evidence indicates names of an expert, his or her mission as well as the time limit within which he or she must submit his or her report to the competent organ.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :62
original-oid 07f146aeaa8a940a5db3078bea2ca457b40d678b
data 699
Article 62: Notification of appointment of an expert and commencement of duties
(1) The competent organ notifies the expert of the decision appointing him or her within a period not exceeding three working days from the day the decision is rendered.
(2) The expert, through legal communication, informs the competent organ that he or she has accepted or refused the mission within the time limit set by the competent organ.
(3) Before discharging the duties related to his or her mission, the expert may inform the competent organ and the parties of the place, date and time he or she will begin his or her duties.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :63
original-oid f11c12692967f27d3d5cfd96b960c91af47d6cbd
data 231
Article 63: Grounds for expert disqualification
A party may disqualify an expert on the same grounds as applicable to disqualification of a judge.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :64
original-oid 3344f02f7a350bab041415d0d3881b96ede3dc6e
data 504
Article 64: Time limit for submitting application for disqualification of an expert
An application for disqualification of an expert is submitted before the expert has started the duties related to his or her mission. However, an interested party can disqualify an expert after he or she has started the duties related to his or her mission if the reason for the disqualification has come to his or her knowledge later.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :65
original-oid 5286be6f9221a9ed865a6d5ea4f14c96a7a492c3
data 411
Article 65: Self disqualification
An expert with any reason for self- disqualification or other reason preventing him or her from carrying out the mission assigned to him or her, must immediately inform the competent organ. If necessary, he or she also informs the parties of such a reason and disqualifies himself or herself.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :66
original-oid 0b5591766c2e49a0bd48141e365952c3b13c0694
data 469
Article 66: Procedure for disqualification of an expert
(1) The party alleging that he or she has reasons for disqualifying an expert does so in a written application addressed to the competent organ that appointed him or her.
(2) The application for disqualification of an expert is submitted within one day from the date on which the party knew the reasons for the disqualification.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :67
original-oid 927d16d0f70e63fb69a99639bc63acbca0a32b67
data 1096
Article 67: Notification of the disqualification of an expert
(1) Before examining the reasons for disqualification, the competent organ examines the admissibility of application for disqualification.
(2) If the competent organ finds that the application for disqualification is admissible, it sends a copy of the application for disqualification to the disqualified expert and informs the expert that he or she is required to declare his or her opinion thereon within two working days from receipt of the letter notifying the disqualification.
(3) After the expert gives or does not give his or her opinion on the reasons for disqualification indicated by a party, the competent organ makes a decision within three working days.
(4) If the disqualification is unfounded, the competent organ notifies the expert to continue his or her duties and informs the parties accordingly.
(5) In case the competent organ upholds the disqualification, another expert is appointed in accordance with provisions of this Law.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :68
original-oid c092ef58377af2f1070b92b0cfdfef1d229db88c
data 640
Article 68: Collaboration between an expert and a party
(1) The party submits anything required by the expert to enable him or her to carry out his or her mission.
(2) If any party fails to respect the provisions of Paragraph (1) of this Article, the expert informs in writing the competent organ. After giving the parties the opportunity to defend themselves, the competent organ may order them the submission to the expert of any necessary requirements for discharging his or her mission or authorise the expert to submit his or her report as it stands.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :69
original-oid 1b6069403fa9f01b933b9db408aa19a16eda428b
data 395
Article 69: Scope of an expert mission
An expert gives a report including a conclusion on issues solely relating to his or her mission as entrusted by the competent organ. However, before submission of the report, the expert or any of the parties may petition the competent organ to widen the expert's mission.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :70
original-oid 59386e97ab4b4004a1fc26ec4916e90eb00b54ec
data 524
Article 70: Time for submission of an expert report
(1) An expert submits his or her report within the time limit set in the decision to appoint him or her. However, an expert may request in writing for the extension of the time for submission of the report and motivate his or her request.
(2) If the competent organ considers that the grounds are well-founded, it grants the expert an extension of the deadline for submitting the report.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :71
original-oid ffa4a6883f7c6f4050a5fe8b61a065a4661f3a65
data 454
Article 71: Replacement of an expert
(1) Whenever it is necessary, the competent organ, on its own initiative, may replace an expert.
(2) An interested party may request in writing the competent organ to replace an expert and provide reasons thereof.
(3) The procedure for replacing an expert is the same as the one for appointing an expert provided for under this Law.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :72
original-oid 5cf5ff5ae7c847e242e3d6ae2a6188c7cdbd78ea
data 561
Article 72: Report of the expert
(1) The expert, at the end of his or her mission, submits to the competent organ a report of his or her achievements.
(2) The report of the expert bears the fingerprint or signature of the expert preceded by the following oath:
"I, ………, swear that I have fulfilled my mission completely, independently, honourably and conscientiously, with accuracy and probity. May I face the rigors of the law if I did not do it with the due accuracy".
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :73
original-oid 7ee72e615b46436357601612d2150f6de46aec67
data 432
Article 73: Experts' disagreement on the conclusion
If several experts are appointed, they make a single report for submission to the competent organ and agree on the same conclusion according to the opinion of the majority. If they disagree on the conclusion, the report includes reasons for disagreement without specifying each expert's opinion.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :74
original-oid 902f271fc412a0d04f68665860ea63eef6722482
data 831
Article 74: Request for additional explanations from an expert
(1) If the competent organ is not satisfied with explanations in the report of the expert, it may order the expert to give additional explanations. However, if the competent organ finds that a new expert evidence is required, it may request it from that expert or from another expert. The new expert may request the predecessor for explanations he or she thinks are necessary.
(2) The competent organ may hear the expert during the hearing of the claim after he or she takes oath. The expert may consult documents in the course of this hearing session. The statements of the expert must be recorded in minutes and bear the fingerprint or the signature of the expert after reading it.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :75
original-oid 6b697909ead6aaf44041c4aabfa2a9c9d8f1e446
data 384
Article 75: Failure of the expert to submit a report
If an expert fails, without reason, to submit the report of the mission assigned to him or her within the time limit fixed or extended by the competent organ, the competent organ takes a decision to dismiss him or her and appoints another expert.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :76
original-oid b851754733011a8efc51eab82b1f0cc1d8e54713
data 361
Article 76: Participation of an expert in an investigation
If the competent organ considers that an investigation is necessary, it may decide that an expert should participate in the investigation to provide clarification based on knowledge of the problem under consideration.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :77
original-oid 132492fdf6d1cb3806f131edc4962905ee8f9fe3
data 422
Article 77: Challenging an expert report
(1) When the competent organ considers that the expert report does not enable to solve a problem, it may, on its own initiative, decide the appointment of another expert.
(2) A party who disagrees with the expert report on valid grounds, may request the competent organ to appoint another expert.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :78
original-oid 2fda90b5273ef302a52dfbeda8b21b7e516f7bc1
data 333
Article 78: Probative value of the expert report
The competent organ is not bound to follow an expert's opinion. However, it considers the expert report and attributes probative value to the content thereof depending on the matter being considered.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :79
original-oid fa56435763265eec2966eeac14a22a2174ff9c6b
data 270
Article 79: Explanations of an expert at the appeal level
The competent organ may, at the appeal level, summon an expert to give verbal explanations on a report placed in the case file.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :80
original-oid 76e35d37d4b6c5821c77c9a5ae8ff83ec7bf6c28
data 437
Article 80: Oath of an expert preparing to give explanations
When an expert prepares to give explanations before the competent organ, he first takes the following oath:
"I, ……………….…., swear to fulfil my mission in honour and conscience, with accuracy and probity. May I face the rigors of the law if I do not do it with the due accuracy".
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 4: Expert evidence

blob
mark :81
original-oid e6e1f07022b98e1c92d55a60979637cf2e9757f7
data 286
Article 81: Admissibility of human presumption
The competent organ admits the human presumption only if it is relevant, precise and consistent and in such case only as the law admits evidence by any means.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 5: Presumption

blob
mark :82
original-oid 53348979e78a0588ebdc5455190bb5f9335c26c1
data 498
Article 82: Claim preclusion
(1) Claim preclusion is immutable and is attached only to what has been decided.
(2) Before claim preclusion can be given effect, the following conditions must be met:
(a) the subject matter of the claim must be the same;
(b) the claim must be based on the same cause;
(c) the dispute or the case must be between the same parties and brought by them and against them in the same capacity.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 5: Presumption

blob
mark :83
original-oid 46ae2b423afaddc57eb5762a20b252e00d7fed3a
data 355
Article 83: Exemption from production of evidence additional to legal presumption
(1) A legal presumption relieves the party for whose benefit it exists of the burden of proving it.
(2) No evidence is admissible against a legal presumption, unless otherwise provided by law.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 5: Presumption

blob
mark :84
original-oid 12cb4b5568ea1539ae7233487845f86fef73eac3
data 348
Article 84: Probative value of oral admission made out of the competent organ
An allegation of oral admission made outside of the competent organ is not considered as valid admission of the author if it is a case in which testimonial evidence is not allowed.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 6: Admission by a party

blob
mark :85
original-oid 95f391306933181e39c24a149c265634b1ecba83
data 930
Article 85: Probative value of admission made before the competent organ
(1) The admission made by a party before the competent organ is valid only when either of the following conditions is met:
(a) the defendant clearly understands the nature and consequences of the admission of the claim;
(b) the admission is voluntarily made by the defendant;
(c) the admission is supported by other evidence.
(2) Omitting certain statements for in a confession in order to consider them valid is not authorized.
(3) A party who admits allegations made against him or her under the conditions referred to in Paragraph (1) of this Article may not revoke his or her admission unless he or she proves that it was the result of physical duress, deception or error of fact. He or she may not revoke his or her admission under the ground of an error of law.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 6: Admission by a party

blob
mark :86
original-oid 6a5a3d3c668fccc6ecd24543b9b484a22a18a3a5
data 280
Article 86: Admission of one or several co- debtors
Admission of one or several co-debtors made before or outside the competent organ does not bind his or her co-debtor or other co- debtors.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: EVIDENCE IN CIVIL MATTERS
Section: Section 6: Admission by a party

blob
mark :87
original-oid d4378b68fa31c47b936a5d81ecf152d12b551691
data 225
Article 87: Evidence in criminal matters
In criminal matters, evidence is established by any means of fact or law, provided that parties are given time for cross-examination.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: EVIDENCE IN CRIMINAL MATTERS

blob
mark :88
original-oid c1969639c37e6fb0160a230ca30a20a0bc148700
data 253
Article 88: Application of evidence used in civil matters to criminal matters
Without prejudice to the provisions of Article 87 of this Law, evidence in civil matters may also apply to criminal matters.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: EVIDENCE IN CRIMINAL MATTERS

blob
mark :89
original-oid e5a662f6477272136fbcb1e4934be08b8ca87f01
data 587
Article 89: Matters to which material evidence relates
(1) Material evidence may relate to-
(a) an object or trace found on the accused or the victim;
(b) an object or trace found in possession of the accused;
(c) an object or trace found in possession of the victim;
(d) an object or trace found in possession of a third party related to the accused;
(e) an object or trace found at the crime scene or elsewhere.
(2) Material evidence does not relate to a document. However, a document can sometimes be considered as material evidence.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: EVIDENCE IN CRIMINAL MATTERS

blob
mark :90
original-oid 421fe3047222e751d7927ab2a856492a8d8d234f
data 442
Article 90: Indication of the origin of material evidence
A party who produces material evidence must establish its origin in order to link it to the commission of offence. Also, he or she must establish that the evidence is indeed the one that was seized or collected at the time of occurrence of the facts and demonstrate that it remained unaltered despite manipulations carried out on it.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: EVIDENCE IN CRIMINAL MATTERS

blob
mark :91
original-oid 2df03bf8800ad70a3aaded25f14c8cc698cfd450
data 278
Article 91: Explanations on drawings sketch
A drawing, sketch or any other similar representation must be accompanied by explanations and the competent organ may request the person who drew it to provide interpretation thereon.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: EVIDENCE IN CRIMINAL MATTERS

blob
mark :92
original-oid 7f79e889f47e7096db1ce4e85c785401fa33b28e
data 1245
Article 92: Procedures for investigating and visiting the location of the subject matter in dispute
(1) At any time prior to concluding the consideration of the dispute, the competent organ may, on its own initiative or at the request of a party, order an investigation. Also, the competent organ may decide to conduct its own investigation and thus visit the location of the subject matter in dispute.
(2) During the investigation, one or all of the judges who are members of the bench hearing the case or determining the dispute may visit the location of the subject matter in dispute.
(3) The competent organ's decision ordering the investigation specifies the day and time when the competent organ has to arrive at the place of investigation. However, the decision of the competent organ may not specify the day and time by which he must arrive at the place of investigation if such specification is likely to result in the alteration, concealment or destruction of the evidence sought.
(4) The persons involved affix their fingerprint or signature to the report mentioning the elements examined. If a person refuses or is unable to affix their fingerprint or signature, this is noted.
Chapter: CHAPTER V: MISCELLANEOUS AND FINAL PROVISIONS

blob
mark :93
original-oid f3aa848b78aaf70d8e01f496429b417d90d46505
data 722
Article 93: Cooperation in investigation
(1) A court may delegate to another Rwandan court equal in rank to it or lower in rank than it the performance of an act that it specifies in relation to the subject matter in dispute, unless that court has ever heard the dispute.
(2) The delegated court is required to perform the delegated act within the limits of the delegation and within the time limit set by the delegating court, unless a reason arises which makes it impossible to meet the time limit.
(3) A Rwandan court may, through the President of the Supreme Court, delegate an act to another foreign court or respond to a question addressed to it by the latter.
Chapter: CHAPTER V: MISCELLANEOUS AND FINAL PROVISIONS

blob
mark :94
original-oid 253ee31146d1374c205c9a21005869706258fa26
data 148
Article 94: Language provision
This Law was drafted, considered and adopted in Ikinyarwanda.
Chapter: CHAPTER V: MISCELLANEOUS AND FINAL PROVISIONS

blob
mark :95
original-oid a61c0e6b7423fd1a0d02aadbae0bf359c39e5b74
data 169
Article 95: Repealing provision
Law n° 15/2004 of 12/6/2004 relating to evidence and its production is repealed.
Chapter: CHAPTER V: MISCELLANEOUS AND FINAL PROVISIONS

blob
mark :96
original-oid 2b1625ec25ab24396e73930e224aff52a10e9bbe
data 192
Article 96: Entry into force
This Law comes into force on the date of its publication in the Official Gazette of the Republic of Rwanda.
Chapter: CHAPTER V: MISCELLANEOUS AND FINAL PROVISIONS

