mark :378
original-oid 7bc9d7cf4962bdc0a838a9206784ab12bc1599aa
data 331
Article 113: Trying a civil action
When a civil action is instituted before a criminal court, the court hears such action in accordance with Laws governing civil procedure for cases heard on the merits.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 4: Civil action arising from an offence

blob
mark :379
original-oid 1efaa95d9565416c6fb33ef64adfef9c290e96d6
data 1073
Article 114: Separating a civil action from a criminal action
In case a civil action is filed before a criminal court, the court may, at its own initiative, or upon request by one of the parties, separate a civil action from the criminal case in case the civil action may prejudice or delay the trial of the criminal action.
The decision to separate the civil action from the criminal action is an administrative decision which may be taken by a court registrar or a judge on own initiative or on request of one of the parties. The decision, upon application to the President of the Court, may be reversed within forty-eight (48) hours in case it is taken by the registrar. In that case, the proceedings continue after the decision is taken.
A decision to separate cases taken by a judge during the hearing is not subject to appeal.
However, separation of a civil action from a criminal action at first instance at appeal level is inadmissible.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 4: Civil action arising from an offence

