mark :389
original-oid 8226c180239258862af4bf27ccf4d37435f985ec
data 475
Article 124: Appearance of a person liable for damages and claimant for damages
The offender and the civil party appear before court in person or represented.
However, a court can, at any time, order a party to appear in person.
An order that requires a party to appear in person and the date of appearance are notified in accordance with provisions on summons.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 5: Appearance of parties

blob
mark :390
original-oid ba78c137b3394d3332400a96bd0e6d52bf109724
data 1991
Article 125: Preliminary hearing
The panel can, if it considers it necessary, and after consultations with the President of the Court, confirm that the preliminary hearing precedes the trial of the case and determine the date of hearing.
Preliminary hearing is conducted in camera. The court sets the agenda of the preliminary hearing. The court does this after consultations with the concerned parties.
During preliminary hearing, the court can examine issues related to admission of the case and other incidental proceedings that can hinder or delay the trial on the merits.
Matters that are examined in the preliminary hearing include:
1° matters related to jurisdiction, interests and capacity;
2° matters on security of witnesses;
3° matters on separation or merger of cases;
4° matters based on the right to representation;
5° the number of witnesses, their role, their location and the means of application in their communication;
6° confirming the witnesses that require protection and to put in place security strategies for the authorized parties;
7° confirmation of the number and identity of witnesses expected to be summoned by the public prosecution and those to be summoned by the defendant;
8° to agree on arguments of the case, their supporting evidence and the legal provisions of both parties on each argument;
9° to agree on the schedules of the proceedings depending on the arguments of the case;
10° to agree on the time each party will utilize in explaining every item and to hear its witnesses;
11° to inquire from both parties the time when hearing the case in merits would commence and end;
12° admission of testimony from the person who will not be present during the hearing and such testimony can also be recorded through audio-visual format;
13° to take any other necessary decisions that are helpful in the smooth conduct of the hearing.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 6: Pre-trial session

blob
mark :391
original-oid 3352993f54a95f88755657455576edd3ccacc986
data 633
Article 126: Guiding principles of criminal cases
Criminal cases must adhere to the following substantial principles:
1° being held in public;
2° being fair and impartial;
3° respect for the right to defence and to legal counsel;
4° adversarial proceedings and equality of parties before the law;
5° basing on evidence lawfully produced and being rendered within the time limits prescribed by Law in the language used in the pleading.
The prosecution procedure is carried out and completed in accordance with law.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :392
original-oid 28b0c736ce053fedf4a9373942a37286637689c6
data 3095
Article 127: Procedure for conducting the hearing
The hearing is conducted in the following order:
1° the president of the bench announces the commencement of the hearing;
2° the court registrar calls upon the parties and reads out the initial charges;
3° the judge examines whether the identities of the parties are complete and agrees with the parties on their particulars;
4° the judge examines the identities of the witnesses, if present, and asks the parties if there are witnesses that are not accepted and confirms those who will be heard, puts them in private and separates them;
5° the judge together with the parties examines any incidentals that may have not been cleared in the preliminary hearing and takes a decision thereon. No any other incidentals are admitted unless those based on public order which can be admitted at any time;
6° in case there is no incidental, the judge describes briefly what constitutes submissions from parties and announces the main points of the case and the order in which they will be examined in accordance with what was agreed in the preliminary hearing;
7° the judge asks the prosecutor to indicate the facts of the case and evidence on each point in accordance with the order of the hearing, and if necessary, he calls upon the witnesses to testify;
8° on the other side, the judge asks the defendant and defence counsels to present their defence and where necessary, the defence witnesses are given the opportunity as well;
9° the judge himself or herself examines each party;
10° parties or counsels can cross-examine witnesses of the other party. Where necessary, the prosecution or the defendant can re-examine the witnesses invited in the court;
11° the judge can, at any time, during presentation of evidence and examination of witnesses ask questions that can assist him or her to understand the submissions of parties and to examine whether the evidence and their legal basis as presented by parties in regard to each point in the case are understood in a clear and similar manner, whether on the side of the judge as well as on the side of the parties;
12° in case there are witnesses, they are examined on individual basis after taking oath. If necessary, they are given an opportunity to cross-examine each other in front of the parties or in absence of parties, if their presence can prejudice truth;
13° parties are given an opportunity to respond on the testimony, and where necessary, to cross-examine each other or between them and witnesses;
14° the judge, after hearing each party on all points of the case, declares the hearing closed and informs parties the date and time of pronouncement of the judgment;
15° parties sign on the hearing minutes;
A judge, at all times, refrain from engaging in questions that can demonstrate partiality or questions that can indicate the direction of the case before the final judgment.
The presiding judge is responsible for conducting and closing the hearing.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :393
original-oid 5cb60d6069817dc8eb0fc567ca487103b1af9aef
data 858
Article 128: Trial in absentia or in the absence of the public prosecution
If a person duly summoned fails to appear without valid reasons, he or she is tried in absentia.
If the public prosecution does not appear for the first time, the case is adjourned. If the public prosecution does not appear for the second time with no reason and when the defendant is in provisional detention, the court orders provisional release of the defendant and the case is heard in absentia of the public prosecution unless the defendant is accused of a felony. In case the public prosecution fails to appear for three (3) consecutive times in regard to a felony, the hearing continues while the defendant is free even in the absence of the public prosecution.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :394
original-oid e001fcac36f7ef426fceab16619c16bac25e7e8e
data 1123
Article 129: Amicus curiae
A person or an organ can be authorized to provide submissions as amicus curiae if they have expertize that can assist the court in taking a decision on the case.
Application for being amicus curiae is done in writing and addressed to the president of the competent court.
The application must indicate a detailed identity and the document indicating impartiality of the applicant, a document of expertise in the field, a brief description of issues in respect of which the applicant deems it necessary to provide assistance and justify the usefulness of his or her assistance in the effective delivery of justice;
The application of the amicus curiae is rejected if the amicus curiae does not provide any assistance in the case or if there is a reasonable ground to indicate partiality.
The court can, for purposes of smooth running of the case, invite a person or an organ as amicus curiae to provide any information, expertise or any other thing the court can consider necessary.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :395
original-oid b8277e3fe6e76f81304bd8dc12131f9236daaca6
data 425
Article 130: Time and place for the conduct of hearing
The hearing takes place at the seat of the court on the date set by the court. If necessary, the hearing can take place in such other place as can be determined by the court.
The court can also conduct hearings and hear witnesses through electronic means.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :396
original-oid 93d5132879ec7a6f31fc29f1a2899b0032f22fd7
data 574
Article 131: Order of the court to conduct hearing in camera
Hearing is conducted in public. However, the court can decide to conduct the hearing in camera if the hearing can endanger public order or public morals and at any time the judge believes it is necessary.
In case hearing in camera is decided, pronouncement of decisions on objections and incidental proceedings can also be conducted in camera.
The judgment on merits is always pronounced in public.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :397
original-oid ea5b8e8e3372af5b2c99b969412f34d91154b14f
data 396
Article 132: Records by the court registrar
The court registrar takes note of the proceedings, particulars of parties and witnesses as well as their key submissions and they affix their signature thereto.
Other electronic means can be used to record court proceedings, if possible.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :398
original-oid 741545ca2121d1453738dd49ed95d2b5aaceb3a5
data 1047
Article 133: Conduct required of a person speaking at the hearing
In his or her acts and words, any person speaking at the hearing must be respectful towards judges, adversarial parties and all other people present.
A party who takes floor at the hearing speaks freely about matters relating to the case but with all due respect to justice and clarity to enable judges to easily understand his or her contentions.
A party who diverts and speaks issues irrelevant to the subject matter or who demonstrates minimal politeness is called to order by the presiding judge. If the party persists or uses disrespectful language, the presiding judge silences the party for a moment in a polite way without assaulting or upsetting him or her. If the party refuses to comply, he or she is punished in accordance with the law.
Any order of the judge meant to maintain order for the smooth running of the hearing is immediately executed as such.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :399
original-oid f9363c871dc994296ce3ca6ac9099c1cd7a295e4
data 373
Article 134: Dress code that conforms to the honour of the court
Any dress code that undermines the court honour during court proceedings is prohibited.
Instructions of the President of the Supreme Court identifies prohibited dress code in the court hearing.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :400
original-oid 1b0a6de04e0587a524e3c525fee1c80097642cd3
data 1087
Article 135: Order during the hearing
In a court or in any other place where a public court hearing is held, if one or more persons present make noise, express approval or disapproval by causing or stirring up trouble in any way, the presiding judge calls them to order and expel them if they persist and, if necessary, seek intervention of law enforcement forces without prejudice to other penalties provided by law.
If the person who causes trouble is a party to the proceedings, the presiding judge call him or her to order and warn him or her that if he or she persists, he or she will expel him or her and continue the hearing as if he or she is present. If he or she is expelled and refuses to leave the courtroom, the presiding judge seeks intervention of law enforcement forces to expel the party by force without prejudice to other penalties provided by the law.
If the trouble is caused by a court employee, the provisions of Paragraph One of this Article apply.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :401
original-oid 06aeef484e9bdd554bc5663991a17b96cc1a2eb1
data 1072
Article 136: Audio and visual recording in court hearing
Except persons authorized by the President of the court, any other person who intends to take audio or visual records in the court does so before the panel of judges enters the court room or after the closure of hearing and after the panel of judges exits the court room.
However, any person who intends to write anything relating to the hearing is authorized to do so.
Authorisation to take audio or visual records in court hearing is applied for in writing at least forty eight (48) hours before the hearing.
The President of the court takes a decision to accept or reject the application to take audio or visual records before the hearing after examining whether there are no consequences on justice, national security, security of the parties and public morals. The decision is notified to the applicant who requested authorisation to take audio or visual records before the beginning of hearing.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :402
original-oid eccf6296b07b267148f29da5da4281e25ee1bcff
data 613
Article 137: Trial of offences of contempt of court
If a person commits an offence of contempt of court punishable with a maximum of imprisonment of five (5) years, the court can immediately sentence him or her.
If a person commits an offence of contempt of court punishable with imprisonment of more than five (5) years, the court, after ordering for his or her arrest and drawing up a statement of facts, produces him or her before a Prosecutor and submits incriminating evidence for prosecution.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 7: Conduct of the hearing

blob
mark :403
original-oid 0cfc7a38be75ad8df937819ed3365a8d92ca6f2d
data 795
Article 138: Time for pronouncement of judgment
Every judgment must be in writing and pronounced in a period not exceeding thirty (30) days from the day of closure of the hearing. In case of failure, a judge or judges who heard the case are subject to disciplinary measures.
Reasons that led to failure of pronouncement of judgment within thirty (30) days are indicated in case judgment.
In case one of the parties or his or her counsel was not informed of the day of pronouncement of the judgment, he or she is informed by the court registrar or court bailiff of how the case was decided in accordance with the usual regulations governing notification of court documents.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 8: Pronouncement of the judgement

blob
mark :404
original-oid b14fdd22c6a2fca8f77e3a24b87a574e86de73d3
data 1806
Article 139: Contents of the judgment
A court judgment must indicate the following:
1° the number of the case;
2° the court that heard the case, place and date on which the judgment was pronounced;
3° complete identity of parties;
4° subject matter;
5° nature of the case;
6° list of issues that constitute the case;
7° examination of each issue in the case indicating the submissions of each party and the decision of the court on each issue on the basis of fact and law;
8° decision of the court whether the accused is innocent or guilty;
9° the offence of which the party is convicted if he or she was not innocent;
10° awarded penalties;
11° damages that must be paid, if any;
12° decision taken on the seized property;
13° whether parties were present or not;
14° whether the hearing was conducted in public or in camera and whether the judgment was pronounced in public;
15° the date and place where the case was decided;
16° the name or names of the judges who heard the case;
17° whether it is decided and pronounced in public;
18° whether pronouncement was postponed and reasons thereof;
19° any dissenting opinion and reasons thereof in brief;
20° names of judge or judges who heard the case;
21° names of the court registrar.
If the case was decided by more than one judge, any judge or judges who disagree with the decision taken by colleagues can attach their dissenting opinion and the reasons to the judgment and that opinion is written below signatures of the members of bench. The dissenting opinion is not read in public.
The case is signed by the judge or judges who heard the case and the court registrar who was present during pronouncement.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 8: Pronouncement of the judgement

blob
mark :405
original-oid 67a983c329652c415f0e6138b2a5e3286e94d90b
data 585
Article 140: Fate of bail in case of acquittal or conviction of the accused
If the accused had been granted bail and is acquitted, the court orders that such bail be refunded to him or her after possible deduction of additional court fees incurred as a result of his or her absence in a court proceedings.
If the accused is convicted, the bail in whole or in part is paid out for redress for the damages caused by the offence, restitutions, fines and court fees.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 8: Pronouncement of the judgement

