data 306
Article 142: Notification of a judgment pronounced in absentia
The defendant is notified of the judgment pronounced in absentia in procedures similar to procedures of serving summons.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: PROSECUTION OF THE ACCUSED AT THE FIRST INSTANCE
Section: Section 8: Pronouncement of the judgement

blob
mark :408
original-oid 4e0c09ff5d4685ed12ab1663881ade94b69ee3e2
data 1054
Article 143: Unlawful detention
Any detention in violation of provisions of Articles 66, 74 and 79 of this Law is unlawful and punishable.
Unlawful detention provided under Paragraph One of this Article includes:
1° detaining a person in an irrelevant facility;
2° detaining a person for a period longer than the period specified in the arrest statement and in the provisional detention warrants;
3° continued detention of a person after a decision rejecting provisional detention or its extension or granting provisional release was taken;
4° continued detention of a person after a decision of acquittal was taken;
5° continued detention of a person who was punished by a fine;
6° detaining a person whose sentence was suspended;
7° continued detention of a person who served his or her sentence;
8° being detained by an unauthorized person;
9° detention that does not comply with formalities of arrest and provisional detention.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section One: Procedure for trying a case on unlawful detention

blob
mark :409
original-oid 35fd8e274493aff2a0770df39e40031ea0454230
data 1701
Article 144: Procedure for instituting a claim against unlawful detention
Any person who files a case claiming of his or her illegal detention or that of a third party does so in the court competent to hear such a case which is near the place of detention, in accordance with the normal way of filing a direct case.
The claim is heard within forty eight (48) hours from the time the claim was instituted and the decision is taken within five (5) days. The claim is not subject to legal fees.
The President of the Court, after the claim is registered, assigns a panel of judges to hear the case and the plaintiff is summoned to present in details the justifications of what is contained in submissions submitted to the Court.
The judge who hears the case mentioned in Paragraph 2 of this Article, after understanding the submissions of the plaintiff and the legal provisions relating to the case, can or cannot summon the defendant.
When the decision to summon the defendant is taken, the defendant appears as an individual or through his or her legal counsel together with the person he or she is accused to have been unlawfully detained.
The decision referred to in Paragraph 4 of this Article provides justifications for summoning the defendant, errors of law and date of hearing and orders the defendant to submit to the court defence submissions in a period not exceeding twenty four (24) hours before the hearing.
The person who took an illegal decision or who failed to take the required order is heard in accordance with law.
The public prosecution must be summoned in such cases.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section One: Procedure for trying a case on unlawful detention

blob
mark :410
original-oid acb479e8162327bef142790b2c58ab8ca01f201a
data 707
Article 145: Decision on claim against unlawful detention
The court can, after considering the justifications provided, order for the retaining or release of the detainee. The court can also order for provisional release of the detainee and instruct him or her to comply with requirements specified in Article 80 of this Law.
Where the court convicts the person who is accused of unlawful detention, it sentences him or her to the penalties provided for by the Law.
The court order can be subject to appeal within the time limits provided by the procedure on provisional detention and release.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section One: Procedure for trying a case on unlawful detention

blob
mark :411
original-oid 8382a6f9e0badc7dd2a18983ccadfb4e916ce882
data 454
Article 146: Determination of minority of a juvenile offender
The time of commission of an offence serves as the basis for the determination of the minority of a juvenile offender.
A juvenile offender prosecuted by the public prosecution is tried by the specialised chamber for minors even if the trial is conducted after he or she attains the majority age.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 2: Prosecution of a juvenile offender

blob
mark :412
original-oid 028508d6858bfc6f54c97c2075a1d34e51f14795
data 1037
Article 147: Prosecuting a juvenile offender
An investigator or a prosecutor in charge of the case file does everything possible and conducts the necessary investigations to establish the truth about the personality of the juvenile offender and identify appropriate means for his or her rehabilitation.
The investigator or the prosecutor, through investigations, gathers all evidence concerning all the aspects of the minor's life including character and history, educational background, school behaviour and conditions relating to upbringing of the child.
A prosecutor can order a medical examination and, where applicable, require a psychological examination for the minor. The prosecutor decides to put the minor in a conducive atmosphere to enable the minor be rehabilitated for social reintegration.
Where necessary, the prosecutor can issue all necessary warrants or order judicial supervision in compliance with rules of general law.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 2: Prosecution of a juvenile offender

blob
mark :413
original-oid f74d8b1c1211d4192ed50ac1644278210b6c9438
data 579
Article 148: Trial of a minor and his or her co-accused
A minor aged above fourteen (14) years but below eighteen (18) years is tried in accordance with rules of general law.
A specialized chamber for minors adjudicates the case after hearing the minor, witnesses, his or her parents, guardian or custodian, the public prosecution and his or her legal counsel. It also tries his or her co-accused or accomplices having reached the age of majority, unless the law provides otherwise.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 2: Prosecution of a juvenile offender

blob
mark :414
original-oid d7a16416f913deabf1481ff4f1a07220317fa8a0
data 350
Article 149: Detention of a minor under prosecution
A minor aged below fourteen (14) years is not prosecuted.
A juvenile offender aged fourteen (14) years is subject to prosecution but cannot be detained in the same detention facility as major suspects.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 2: Prosecution of a juvenile offender

blob
mark :415
original-oid 8e50d891b18175cf161baf5c6ba6f65bf83418b4
data 583
Article 150: Decision that can be taken against a juvenile offender
After compliance with the procedure provided for under Article 147 of this Law, the prosecutor can:
1° release the minor if he or she considers that there is no sufficient incriminating evidence against the child and return him or her to the parents, guardians or custodians;
2° take the juvenile offender to a competent court;
3° put the minor in a public centre intended for that purpose pending a court decision.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 2: Prosecution of a juvenile offender

blob
mark :416
original-oid 39e7690721a5a10c84742a38f85729adc31900ef
data 662
Article 151: Right to legal counsel
A juvenile offender under prosecution must have a legal counsel from investigations stage to court proceedings. If the juvenile offender or his or her guardians are unable to access a legal counsel, the organ in charge of investigations or the public prosecution requests the President of the Bar Association to assign a legal counsel to the juvenile.
If the public prosecution does not seek legal representation for the juvenile, the judge requests the President of the Bar Association to assign a legal counsel to the juvenile.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 2: Prosecution of a juvenile offender

blob
mark :417
original-oid d70d910f85e83bb8c7f30317c6172477839b7558
data 336
Article 152: Measure taken by the specialised chamber for minors
A specialised chamber for minors, depending on the case filed, takes measures for the protection, assistance, supervision and education of the minor it considers appropriate.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 2: Prosecution of a juvenile offender

blob
mark :418
original-oid 76ff5f68a794069751a2d671a1136fc4ac50ec3b
data 693
Article 153: Civil action against a minor
A civil action against a minor and the person with civil responsibility over the minor is filed in the specialised chamber for minors.
If one or several minors are jointly charged with one or several adults, a claim for damages is filed in the court competent to try adults. In that case, the minor does not appear in court in person and rather appears through his or her legally recognized representative.
If a minor fails to choose for himself or herself a representative or if the guardian is unable to do so, the juvenile is assigned a legal counsel.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 2: Prosecution of a juvenile offender

blob
mark :419
original-oid 39447f340c6670fa844d604071348be9b9917899
data 552
Article 154: Prosecution of a Rwandan who commits an offence outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda
A Rwandan national who commits a felony or a misdemeanour outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda, can be prosecuted and tried by Rwandan courts in accordance with the Rwandan laws as if the offence was committed on the Rwandan territory if such an offence is punishable under Rwandan law.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 3: Prosecution of an offence committed outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda

blob
mark :420
original-oid 6667f9f6da42f133a98d75a79956a012dabdf396
data 592
Article 155: Prosecution of a felony or a misdemeanour committed abroad
The prosecution of a felony or misdemeanour committed abroad can only be instituted at the request of the public prosecution or any other interested persons.
Prosecution cannot be instituted if the accused proves that he or she was subject to a final judgment delivered abroad and that in the event of conviction he or she served his or her sentence or was granted a pardon.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 3: Prosecution of an offence committed outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda

blob
mark :421
original-oid 52a4a9a68c66ade0e30f4ff98aea98df820cd8a6
data 531
Article 156: Prosecuting an offence committed in no man's land
An offence is considered to have been committed on the territory of the Republic of Rwanda if it is committed by or against a Rwandan national in no man's land or committed by any person on board a vessel flying the Rwanda flag and in waters in a no man's land or on board a Rwanda -registered aircraft in a no man's land.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 3: Prosecution of an offence committed outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda

blob
mark :422
original-oid 238267cfb8da196fb06823b8a4aca925b12835f6
data 537
Article 157: International or transnational crimes
Rwandan courts can prosecute and try any person, whether Rwandan or foreigner, a Rwandan or foreign non-governmental organisation or legal entity that commits, inside or outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda, an international crime or a transnational crime as provided for by the Law determining offences and penalties in general.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 3: Prosecution of an offence committed outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda

blob
mark :423
original-oid b1dc398c9ba58ed97832fd3d29d92a1abea23f22
data 1229
Article 158: Proceedings for international or transnational crimes
In case Rwandan courts try crimes provided for under Article 157 of this Law, the President of the Supreme Court can, in the interest of justice and for the sake of conforming proceedings to the international jurisprudence, seek upon own initiative or request by the accused, his or her legal counsel or by national or foreign public prosecution, judicial cooperation from the United Nations, any other international organisation or a foreign country by requesting them to send judges from other countries to Rwanda to sit alongside Rwandan judges to try cases of international and transnational crimes committed on the Rwandan territory or abroad, of which the transfer to Rwanda is sought and which are provided under the Law determining the jurisdiction of courts.
Such cases are tried both at first instance and appeal level by a bench of at least three (3) judges.
The request for a judge from a foreign country is made in accordance with cooperation procedure between countries and international organisations.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 3: Prosecution of an offence committed outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda

blob
mark :424
original-oid e25419c210e1184a443ec4d578b33a9328f8137f
data 605
Article 159: Prosecution of an offence committed against Rwanda or against a Rwandan national outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda
A Rwandan national or a foreigner who commits a felony or a misdemeanour outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda can be prosecuted and sentenced by Rwandan courts in accordance with the Rwandan laws if the offence is committed against Rwanda or a Rwandan national at the time of the commission of the offence.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 3: Prosecution of an offence committed outside the territory of the Republic of Rwanda

blob
mark :425
original-oid d16020a305170195d3875a37916434e380325662
data 559
Article 160: Fugitive suspect
If the suspect, whether he or she is in Rwanda or in a foreign country, is not arrested because he or she hides or escapes justice, the public prosecution prepares a file for him or her and submits it to the competent court even if the suspect was not interrogated. After receiving the file, the court orders the accused to appear within one (1) month, failing which the court declares that he or she has disobeyed the law.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :426
original-oid 95e5563790ab24fbbfe1e56155ad97c51ef47ddf
data 358
Article 161: Publication of the order
The order provided for under Article 160 of this Law is, within a period of eight (8) days, posted in the appropriate place at the court which issued it and it is published in the newspaper determined by the court.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :427
original-oid 44084f26c47cc6d76b7a86645da721f8332501d9
data 366
Article 162: Trial of a fugitive suspect
If the fugitive suspect fails to respect the time limits provided for under Article 160 of this Law, he or she is tried in absentia.
The court rules on the case based solely on the submissions of the public prosecution.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :428
original-oid c40d13797c4f9404e9632334287621221a1c4b6c
data 224
Article 163: Non representation of a fugitive suspect
No person can legally represent a fugitive suspect in the court.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :429
original-oid da4e07b06d91d735218a36805689e3fe16b0b6df
data 470
Article 164: Confiscation or seizure of the property of a convicted fugitive
If the accused fugitive is found guilty, the court determines modalities for the management of his/her property. The property can be confiscated or seized and sold by auction in accordance with relevant laws, with the proceeds being used to pay for damages to the victims of the offence.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :430
original-oid 10688b3912a7570db65def888e974b10f9ff0867
data 379
Article 165: Publication of the extract of the judgement
The extract of the judgment is posted on the websites of the Judiciary, the Rwanda Public Prosecution Authority and the Rwanda Investigation Bureau and are posted at the notice board of the court that tried the case.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :431
original-oid a51bb90c91c34d055217afdd1e5c6cc467206982
data 348
Article 166: Loss of civil rights
After the publication provided for under Article 165 of this Law is completed, a convicted fugitive is stripped of all civil rights as provided for under the law determining offences and penalties in general.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :432
original-oid b93a9c5b8de27abe5e13bd146822e8bdd43b6cf6
data 317
Article 167: Prosecution of the co-accused of an accused fugitive
The prosecution of the accused fugitive does not in any way whatsoever stay or delay the prosecution of his or her co-accused who are identified.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :433
original-oid ce61a71cecc2a51b28df707d5c8958b7a7a4915d
data 1077
Article 168: Resumption of prosecution of a fugitive
If a convicted fugitive surrenders himself or herself to the judicial police, the Public Prosecution or before the prison administration or if he or she is arrested before the penalty prescribes, the judgement and proceedings conducted from the time the fugitive was ordered to appear until the pronouncement of the judgment become void and the prosecution of the fugitive re-commences in accordance with the ordinary procedure.
If the judgment that had ordered confiscation of his or her property for the benefit of the State or seizure of such property is altered, the property is given back to him or her.
If the property of the convicted person is sold by auction and the person is subsequently acquitted in retrial, the State pays him or her damages equal to the value of his or her property.
If a person is acquitted in retrial of the case in which he or she was convicted, civil parties refund damages received.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :434
original-oid 34e9d1eafac404e9c8ede403f93eaf18bd4f4881
data 539
Article 169: Retrial of a fugitive
In the case provided for under Paragraph One of Article 168 of this Law, if for any reason witnesses fail to attend the new proceedings that are restarted, their written testimonies and written replies of other persons accused of the same offence who were not present during the hearing are read out in the hearing. The same applies to all other necessary documents required to establish the truth.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :435
original-oid 7902d368d593d792fda044afe0cbbf8fcea87442
data 970
Article 170: Persons with privilege of jurisdiction
An investigator or a prosecutor who receives a complaint against or personally observes an offence committed by the President of the Republic, the President of the Senate, the Speaker of the Chamber of Deputies, the President of the Supreme Court or the Prime Minister, immediately transmits the case file to the Prosecutor General who conducts investigation. The Prosecutor General in person prosecutes them before the Supreme Court and in case of his or her absence, he or she is replaced by the Deputy Prosecutor General.
However, accomplices of such persons can be interrogated by an investigator officer or a prosecutor before transmitting the case file to the Prosecutor General.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 5: Prosecution of persons with privilege of jurisdiction and that of Members of Parliament
Subsection: Sub-Section One: Prosecution of persons with privilege of jurisdiction

blob
mark :436
original-oid 6b9feabaf28b3f496febf8cb97d46defb1ef457d
data 1014
Article 171: Grounds for the privilege of jurisdiction and time of consideration
The privilege of jurisdiction serves to protect official responsibilities but not an individual.
If a person holding the office is prosecuted for an offence committed while in the same office, he or she enjoys the privilege of jurisdiction.
If a person is prosecuted for an offence committed before holding an office to which the privilege is attached, he or she enjoys the privilege of jurisdiction if he or she is prosecuted while holding such an office.
If a person who no longer holds an office to which the privilege is attached is prosecuted for an offence committed while holding the same office, he or she loses the privilege of jurisdiction.
ubsection 2: Prosecution of Members of Parliament
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 5: Prosecution of persons with privilege of jurisdiction and that of Members of Parliament
Subsection: Sub-Section One: Prosecution of persons with privilege of jurisdiction

blob
mark :437
original-oid c9b1c29be7934f2c93fc1241b63d8c17a780f934
data 1101
Article 172: Procedure for prosecuting members of Parliament
No member of the Parliament suspected of a felony or misdemeanour can be prosecuted or arrested without the authorisation of the respective Chamber of Parliament to which he or she belongs by voting with a majority of two thirds (2/3) of members present, except where the member of the Parliament is caught red- handed committing a felony or if he or she was definitively convicted.
The Prosecutor General has the power to ask the concerned Chamber of Parliament for authorisation to prosecute one of its members. He or she receives the reply in a period not exceeding fifteen (15) days from the day the concerned chamber received the request. If no response is given within the period specified, the Prosecutor General notifies the President of the Republic in writing who in turn takes a decision thereof.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 5: Prosecution of persons with privilege of jurisdiction and that of Members of Parliament
Subsection: Sub-Section One: Prosecution of persons with privilege of jurisdiction

blob
mark :438
original-oid 268d4f5615a71bdffb093ee2482fecc5240b9f80
data 563
Article 173: Opposition of a case decided in absentia
Opposition seeks to review of a case decided in absentia.
Opposition is instituted by the accused in the court that decided in absentia the case subjected to a file for review. Opposition is instituted in the normal procedure of other complaints.
The submissions for opposition must indicate serious reasons that prevented the defendant from appearing.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection One: Opposition of a case decided in absentia

blob
mark :439
original-oid f6ea848503f96b5f647e6828d762dbf13043735c
data 543
Article 174: Time limits for filing opposition
Opposition is filed in a period of fifteen (15) days from the date of notification of judgment.
If there is no evidence that the person tried in absentia received the judgment, he or she can file for opposition at any time before prescription of the penalty and at any time before the execution of the judgment in relation to civil claims.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection One: Opposition of a case decided in absentia

blob
mark :440
original-oid cafc996f083c9ff6b551498c3620a2bdf2b9890c
data 734
Article 175: Admissibility of an opposition
Opposition is only admissible if the defendant who failed to appear gives a serious reason that prevented him or her from appearing.
The court before which an opposition is filed has full discretion to determine whether the reason given for non-appearance is warranted.
However, a person sentenced by Gacaca courts while abroad is authorized to file for opposition in competent courts when he or she returns in the country. If he or she returns voluntarily, he or she is prosecuted while free until the pronouncement of the judgment.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection One: Opposition of a case decided in absentia

blob
mark :441
original-oid 81dadc1ec81aac590ee8a41efa6c3916c7f1f037
data 470
Article 176: Appearance of a person who files an opposition
A person who files an opposition must appear in person, if he or she was ordered to do so in the judgment rendered in absentia or the judgment rendered in absentia ordered his or her personal appearance as a condition for admissibility of an opposition.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection One: Opposition of a case decided in absentia

blob
mark :442
original-oid 7c4261fc556e880796f6fe9ad42570b49e4edb77
data 382
Article 177: Non-appearance of a person who files an opposition
If a person who files an opposition again fails to appear, such opposition becomes null, unless a serious and exceptional reason for his or her absence is given.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection One: Opposition of a case decided in absentia

blob
mark :443
original-oid 8e37f3cc593e2025b7329439005b4aafc11b6fe5
data 719
Article 178: Interruption of the execution of a judgment in respect of which an opposition was filed
The execution of the judgment in respect of which an opposition is filed must be stayed. However, the court having rendered the judgment in the absence of the accused can order immediate arrest of the person found guilty even though he or she can file an opposition.
The court can also order immediate arrest of the person found guilty when there are serious and exceptional reasons given to the court having rendered the judgment in the absence of the accused.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection One: Opposition of a case decided in absentia

blob
mark :444
original-oid fc3e5892864563f4e93d113be4402f1f9bc65649
data 545
Article 179: Effects of the admissibility of opposition filed by an accused tried in absentia
If an opposition filed by the accused tried in absentia becomes admissible, the judgment in absentia is revoked. The court retries the case in its entirety.
If the case file involves several persons, some of whom have appeared while others have not appeared, all the accused are summoned again.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection One: Opposition of a case decided in absentia

blob
mark :445
original-oid eb8cde21cedcba8eaa784dc015b3708e8858e5f9
data 547
Article 180: Right to appeal
The right to appeal is exercised by the following:
1° a convicted person;
2° a person liable for damages;
3° a civil party or persons who were automatically awarded damages solely for their civil interests;
4° the Public Prosecution.
Except for the Public Prosecution, appeals of the persons under Paragraph One of this Article can be filed by their representatives or by their legal counsels.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :446
original-oid 3bad277d4764a384949152717d214bfaa5ccb652
data 635
Article 181: Time limits for filing an appeal
An appeal must be filed within a period of thirty (30) days from the pronouncement of the judgment with respect to a party that was present or represented at the pronouncement of the judgment. Such time limits also apply to a party duly notified of the date of judgment but fails to appear or to send a representative.
An appeal is filed within a period of thirty (30) days from the date the judgment was notified to the party who was not present at the pronouncement.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :447
original-oid 821b4de8b73713a70a753f16ea900b2949154873
data 210
Article 182: Appeal procedure
An appeal is filed in the same procedure as filing a claim.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :448
original-oid e50e02920b05f290a54969c2613724fe51f39727
data 793
Article 183: Content of written submission in support of appeal
An appeal is filed in the form of a written submission instituting a claim indicating the following:
1° full particulars of the appellant and the accused person on appeal;
2° case under appeal and the court which rendered the judgement;
3° court seized of the appeal;
4° summary of facts;
5° list of defaults of the judgement for which rectification is required and other issues to be considered;
6° explanations for each default or issue showing the mistakes made and how they should be rectified in accordance with laws, evidence and court recommendation;
7° explanations on additional cases if any.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :449
original-oid b1d074a0b010d79ef1532a38dee83ca4fd8dd6a3
data 421
Article 184: Reaction to the written submission in support of appeal
The defendant on appeal prepares a written submission in the same manner as the appellant.
The statement of defence addresses every issue according to the list of raised issues by the appellant and his or her requests to the court.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :450
original-oid e50157cd464426a654add99244009b7d731b0c3b
data 1532
Article 185: Release notwithstanding appeal
The accused held in provisional detention who is acquitted or whose penalty has been suspended or who is only sentenced to pay a fine, is immediately released even if there was an appeal, unless he or she is held in detention for another charge of which he or she is informed and under prosecution in accordance with this Law.
The same applies to the accused held in provisional detention sentenced to a term of imprisonment which is less than or equal to the time spent in provisional detention.
However, the Public Prosecution can, after filing an appeal, petition the court seized at the appeal that the person prosecuted for any of the offences referred to in this Article with compelling evidence that his or her release can disrupt public order be held again in provisional detention until final judgement on appeal.
The offences are the following:
1° genocide;
2° crimes against humanity;
3° war crimes;
4° violence against a minor;
5° crimes against national security or against security of other States;
6° crimes of terrorism;
7° crimes of treason and espionage.
The Public Prosecution makes such a petition in a period of forty eight (48) hours from the date the judgment was pronounced. The court seized at the appeal decides on the petition of the Public Prosecution within forty eight (48) hours from the date and time of receipt of the petition.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :451
original-oid 9cd6d8746465579af89fa13231f003e9a322323c
data 646
Article 186: Accused tried while free
If the accused who is free during the trial is sentenced to imprisonment, he or she remains free when he or she has filed an appeal against the judgement.
However, the court can order his or her immediate arrest if the prosecution has provided sound reasons and parties have discussed such reasons.
However, the court seized at appeal can order the detention of the accused if there are serious and exceptional reasons provided by the Public Prosecution regarding the summary proceeding.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :452
original-oid e95bd1e31ebd7025dda4c5ac4475a1f03bba6a20
data 440
Article 187: Remaining in detention notwithstanding appeal
The accused held in provisional detention before the detention judgement is rendered, remains in detention notwithstanding his or her appeal. However, the court can grant him or her provisional release on serious and exceptional grounds by summary proceedings.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :453
original-oid d091e89f4926164b773089aaddddba33e10bf546
data 758
Article 188: Effects of appeal
The accused can appeal against the whole judgment or some points of judgment of which he or she is not satisfied. The court seized on appeal hears the case only within the limits of the point on appeal.
When a judgment is appealed, its execution is stayed until the expiration of the time limits for appeal or, if the appeal is filed, until the decision on appeal is rendered.
The appeal against the award of damages does not stay the execution of criminal convictions.
If the accused is the only person having filed an appeal, the appellate court cannot increase the penalty imposed in the first instance.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :454
original-oid c265e961feb2900415daa615d936d11f00b4fe04
data 808
Article 189: Withdrawal and writing off an appeal
The Public Prosecution can withdraw the appeal if it considers it unnecessary to prosecute the accused.
The appellant can at any stage of the proceedings withdraw his or her appeal until the proceedings are closed.
An appeal can also be written off if the appellant fails to appear without valid reason or if the appellant fails to request for hearing within six (6) months of the filing of the appeal after failure to appear in a case he or she was summoned in a lawful procedure.
In case of prescription of the criminal action or withdrawal of the appeal by the appellant, the court seized of the appeal confirms the appealed judgment.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :455
original-oid bb69f457dc1ccc57b5a615c245385dee86b33aa7
data 520
Article 190: Appeal against the civil conviction in case of acquittal of the accused
The party claiming for damages appeals only with respect to the civil conviction.
However, the acquittal of the accused in the first instance is not an obstacle for the civil parties to seek damages on appeal. In that case, if the civil party appeals, the appellate court re-examines the entire case on the merits.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :456
original-oid c01d25f9b3148bd8446f9793d1396ed81597bfc9
data 342
Article 191: Appeal of the offender required to pay damages
An offender can appeal against a judgment that ordered him or her to pay damages. His or her appeal is limited to civil matters. The case is re- tried on merits.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :457
original-oid 3ce4a85b6de3b7169fd73d959ada4ead421a743a
data 1051
Article 192: Annulment of an admitted case considered through procedures contrary to the law
In case the appellate court believes that the case decided at first instance through procedures contrary to the law or that the case was filed to a court that lacks appropriate jurisdiction, but the court at first instance decided to admit while it should not admit it, the court seized for appeal admits the appeal and indicate that the case subject to appeal was heard by the first instance court in procedures contrary to law, and annuls all decisions that are based on that error.
If the error is due to lack of jurisdiction, the appealed court sends the file to the competent court. In this case, when the accused is in detention, he or she remains in detention.
If the error is due to another default leading to annulment of judgment, the accused who was in detention is released and any interested person can file the case afresh.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :458
original-oid 0fe787745aba4dd746fcf7d4d4ba1091083e9ce5
data 772
Article 193: Ordering the court to try the case it refused to admit
If the appellate court finds that the case was filed through procedures that comply with the law and the court that tried it at first instance declared that the case was not admitted when it was admissible, the appellate court admits the case and indicates that the case deserved admission at first instance; annuls it and re- submits it to the court that tried it in order to be heard on merits with no any other security on payment of court fees.
The court that receives such a case cannot refuse to hear the case on the pretext that it made a decision on the lack of jurisdiction.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :459
original-oid 5029b848954e5aa6278decbdf6ba9d083b213917
data 350
Article 194: Not using two appeal procedures at the same time
The appellant cannot use, in one case, the appeal and opposition procedures. The first chosen procedure entails the loss of the right to resort to the other procedure.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection 2: Appeal

blob
mark :460
original-oid f218cca3ac3260e57674d4c39fbf629c36c8f843
data 307
Article 195: Definition of review
Review means a procedure that aims to annul a judgment that has become final and re-try the case on the grounds provided under this Law.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :461
original-oid 2b6a2e176d3d0e38fcc9fe29e693cf0351dda733
data 572
Article 196: Persons who can apply for review
Application for review can be filed by the following:
1° Public Prosecution;
2° convicted person;
3° a civil party.
If the convict or the victim of the offence is dead, missing or has disappeared, the application for review can be filed by his or her spouse, child or other legally recognized heirs or legatees or those who received from him or her explicit mission to apply for review.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :462
original-oid 6045bf9a8254fd0c7e005865c365a5c2734096a1
data 1806
Article 197: Grounds for case review
A case can be reviewed due to one of the following grounds:
1° if a person convicted of homicide and later, sufficient evidence is discovered to confirm that the person the convict is alleged to have killed is alive;
2° if, after the accused is convicted of an offence, and it is subsequently found that there is a judgment sentencing another person for the same offence, so that the contradiction between both judgments shows that one of the convicted persons is innocent;
3° if the court finds that corruption was involved in the case and had effects on the judgment;
4° if the judgment was rendered on the basis of documents, testimonies or oaths which later turn out to be or are subsequently declared false by the court after the judgment;
5° if, after the judgment, new conclusive evidence sufficiently showing injustice caused by judgment subject to review is revealed;
6° if the judgment is based on the proceedings conducted on behalf of a person who did not explicitly or implicitly grant permission to do so or prove or confirm such proceedings.
The evidence referred to under item 5o of Paragraph One of this Article is such evidence that the party to the proceedings could not know its existence and that he or she discovered later on or evidence that he or she could not get during the proceedings.
Even when evidence is new, it is taken into account only when it can contradict the basis for the ruling on the case for which review is applied.
Regarding cases heard by a Gacaca Court, case review is accepted only if the convicted person identifies that the person he or she is alleged to have killed is a live.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :463
original-oid 5f96126fd068fe7e81b7db8498f193592bd70dcb
data 428
Article 198: Competent court to review the case
Application for review of a case is lodged in the court that decided the case in the last instance and the case reviewed by a bench of judges that did not try it before.
However, the case heard by a Gacaca Court is reviewed by a Primary Court.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :464
original-oid 85be98963f785a10f4a258227dddf30297d7de3e
data 446
Article 199: Filing an action for case review
The action applying for case review is filed through the same procedure as other actions.
The action applying for case review of a final judgement is not admitted in case it relates to a case for which review was applied on basis of same evidence or same grounds.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :465
original-oid 9f90899353647117cb2d824454c73eacbf9fcb03
data 359
Article 200: Admissibility of case review
The court seized for case review first considers conditions relating to case admissibility and decides on it.
Where a case review is not admitted, the first judgment remains valid.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :466
original-oid f69318743274b434fac0e78ba4d34ae0c4623cfe
data 319
Article 201: Execution of a judgment during case review
Case review does not prevent a judgment from being executed, unless the execution is suspended by the court seized for review.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :467
original-oid 511fa4f244715575b36b6f2e58423a74ed6d2c85
data 300
Article 202: Impossibility of appeal for a ruling made on case review
The judgment of case review does not allow any form of appeal except on grounds of injustice.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :468
original-oid 87110635e385824a33ffd2d08dbdf465ead97e86
data 347
Article 203: Time limit for application for review
The application for review of a final judgement must be filed within two (2) months from the date the event giving rise to the application for review occurred.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :469
original-oid 3c75a34d2584a052ae6f647b4afb7c35f2502362
data 359
Article 204: Language of the court
The language of the court is Ikinyarwanda. However, the court can conduct the hearing in another official language. The party in proceedings can litigate in a language that he or she understands very well and the court seeks an interpreter.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :470
original-oid fe11c77dfe3e017be08d06858792cc0bda70f50c
data 470
Article 205: Translator, expert, interpreter or medical doctor
Any person duly requested to assist as an interpreter, a translator, an expert or a medical doctor can do so if there is nothing to prevent him or her from doing so.
If the person mentioned in Paragraph One of this Article is not a civil servant, he or she is entitled to remuneration proportional to his or her assistance.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :471
original-oid 09c427e6b11175260f2f6818e5e372154ce16fac
data 278
Article 206: Determination of trial costs
An Order of the Minister in charge of justice determines the calculation of fees accorded to witnesses, medical doctors, interpreters and other experts.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :472
original-oid e35f7e88125a31885e2b5ef076272137c8f5c43f
data 899
Article 207: Criteria for determining trial costs
The fees accorded to witnesses, medical doctors and other experts are determined by the bench of judges upon reception of documents containing the following:
1° the day of request for services and the judge who requested for the services;
2° the type of trial and names of parties in the proceedings;
3° the type of services, the date and period of service provision, as well as explanations of service costs.
The court can, if necessary, request the parties in the proceedings to pay the fees needed for remuneration of an expert or an interpreter or it can pay the fees if it appointed him or her.
The fees referred to under Paragraph One of this Article are paid by the party found guilty. If the accused is acquitted, fees are paid out of the State Treasury.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :473
original-oid 148f134ef94d638fbd389105889facc395e65084
data 475
Article 208: Oath of an expert, interpreter, translator or medical doctor
Before commencing his or her duty to render assistance, an expert, interpreter, translator or medical doctor takes the following oath:
"I,................., do hereby swear to perform my duties as faithfully and professionally as possible and in good faith. Should I fail to do so, can I face the rigours of the law."
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :474
original-oid c167f04cef18d95b70253c83e227397f26db1526
data 651
Article 209: Appointment of an interpreter an expert, a translator or a medical doctor
The President of the Court determines, after verification, a person appointed as an interpreter, an expert, a translator, or a doctor. The President can also delegate such powers to other members of staff of the Court under his or her supervision. The person given such powers takes oath provided in Article 208 of this Law.
An interpreter, a translator or another expert who previously took oath does not take oath again if it is necessary for him or her to take the floor again.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :475
original-oid 0eaeffb8b8a7ea38f7ac9323646da35bba378019
data 535
Article 210: Requirements for an expert and his or her report
The person requesting for the assistance of an expert must clearly specify the items he or she expects from the expert.
The report submitted by the expert must give details and the general professional technicalities must be written in a simple language.
The expert, the interpreter, the translator or the medical doctor must make a report according to his or her conscience and expertise.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :476
original-oid 40a58dea31e8b5888563fe0136941b11a42a9d34
data 325
Article 211: Receiving testimony from people other than the suspect
An expert can receive testimony from another person other than the suspect in order for the testimony to act as proof to assist in discharging his or her duties effectively.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :477
original-oid 9bf862f8f2fa66d3638894f382a2eb72d56494f3
data 342
Article 212: Contradictory opinions of several experts
If several experts are appointed and offer contradictory opinions or some of them make reservations to common opinions, each of them gives his or her opinion or gives reasons for his or her reservations.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :478
original-oid aea49dff7ac04be631a935c6319f40526b56f688
data 264
Article 213: Seeking a second opinion from another expert
If a judge has reason to criticize an expert's report, he or she orders a second opinion.
TITLE IV: EXECUTION OF JUDGMENTS
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: USE OF INTERPRETERS, TRANSLATORS, EXPERTS AND MEDICAL DOCTORS

blob
mark :479
original-oid a30c7f56f3dc2658f3a9a73fd4e98cfc6a7d5d19
data 358
Article 214: Execution of judgments
The execution of a criminal judgment is carried out when the judgment has become final unless the court decides otherwise.
A person in charge of the execution of a judgment must have a court document indicating the decision taken.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section One: General provisions

blob
mark :480
original-oid 38ead891c6a8ff50a74a7992bfeb251fb60c8652
data 482
Article 215: Voluntary execution of a judgment
A person sentenced to imprisonment or community service is responsible for executing the judgment by reporting himself or herself to the place of execution.
The voluntary execution of judgment must take place within thirty (30) days from the final court judgment. In case of non-execution, this is considered as insubordination to authorities.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section One: General provisions

blob
mark :481
original-oid fd88264ccf5f317777559408030f1323bd669810
data 813
Article 216: Persons responsible for the execution of judgments
Persons responsible for monitoring the execution of judgments are the following :
1° Rwanda Investigation Bureau, the Public Prosecution or Rwanda National Police, in case of imprisonment penalty and additional penalty;
2° Rwanda Correctional Service, in case of imprisonment penalty;
3° Rwanda National Police or local Government officers empowered to do so by the Minister in charge of justice in case of sentence of community service;
4° a court bailiff, in case of a fine or damages;
5° a court registrar with respect to the collection of fines and court fees, if the property of the sentenced person was placed under the jurisdiction of the court.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section One: General provisions

blob
mark :482
original-oid b022d09b86c9ab0acde916d92c45847526e77131
data 547
Article 217: Release of an accused person who is acquitted
Without prejudice to provisions of Paragraphs 3 and 4 of Article 185 of this Law, an accused who is acquitted is immediately released by the Prison Director upon presentation of a copy of judgment.
If the provisions of Paragraph One of this Article are not enforced by retaining the acquitted person in prison, the judge can take measures provided for under Articles 143, 144 and 145 of this Law.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section One: General provisions

blob
mark :483
original-oid ca3fa299fbb24f40dfa7ece47ba4a053d6e8ce31
data 323
Article 218: Provisional execution of a judgment
Provisional execution of a judgment is prohibited in criminal cases except in matters relating to the restitution of property or payment of damages or when otherwise provided by Law.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section One: General provisions

blob
mark :484
original-oid 61f512defc352ac834cbe946584243e43e26aac2
data 469
Article 219: Foreign criminal judgment
A final foreign criminal judgment can be enforced in Rwanda with regard to penalties at the request of the Public Prosecution.
A final foreign criminal judgment can also be enforced in Rwanda upon request by the victim of the offence if it includes civil damages whose execution must take place on the territory of the Republic of Rwanda.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section One: General provisions

blob
mark :485
original-oid 88ea3cd0f79df46fad691d6aa83310d8c3a72646
data 353
Article 220: Decision of dissolution of a private institution or organisation with legal personality
The decision to dissolve a private institution or organisation with legal personality is transmitted to the competent authority to proceed with the dissolution.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section One: General provisions

