blob
mark :488
original-oid 99e635cd773a7c10e6b949fd98dc7d0bf44dd18c
data 1072
Article 223: Payment of fine and court fees
Fine is paid into the State Treasury within a period determined by the court. Such period cannot exceed one (1) year from the date of final judgment.
Court fees are paid within a period determined by the court. Such period cannot exceed six (6) months from the date of final judgment.
However, the payment of fine and court fees can be ordered to be made immediately after pronouncement of the judgment if both parties are present or from the time of notification to parties when the judgment is pronounced in the absence of either party if there is a reason to fear that the convicted person can escape execution of the penalties.
In that case, the Court Registrar requests the convicted person either in writing or by registered mail to pay fine and court fees within the time limits he or she decides.
The person who paid the fine and court fees submits to the court registrar the certificate of payment of the due fine or court fees.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section 3: Fine and court fees

blob
mark :489
original-oid 8be208eca6a90a7351900c01b4020ea285d4b036
data 471
Article 224: Injunction to pay within the prescribed time limits
The pronouncement of a judgment in the presence of both parties or its notification when pronounced in the absence of either party is considered to be an injunction to pay within the time limits prescribed by the court.
If the convicted person does not pay in the prescribed time limits, he or she is forced to pay.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section 3: Fine and court fees

