blob
mark :535
original-oid 1de13486ade584196124a0b3e821888826486921
data 843
Article 15: Functions of the organ in charge of investigation
(1) Investigators of Rwanda Investigation Bureau perform their functions under the leadership and supervision of the management of the Bureau.
(2) Military investigators work under the leadership and supervision of the Military Prosecutor General.
(3) Non-career investigators are vested with investigation powers by a law or an Order of the Minister in charge of justice. They work under the leadership and supervision of the organs under the authority of which they are. However, when they are performing the investigation function, the provisions of this Law apply.
(4) Investigators perform the investigation function only on offences falling within their subject-matter and territorial jurisdiction.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: INVESTIGATION OF THE ORGAN IN CHARGE OF INVESTIGATION

blob
mark :536
original-oid 12e3d149b2b2c96735985256223eb8028b0c5cca
data 551
Article 16 bis: Preliminary investigations- related acts within the responsibilities of Rwanda National Police
Preliminary investigative acts performed by Rwanda National Police that are provided for by the Law governing Rwanda National Police are handed over to the National Public Prosecution Authority.
Other preliminary investigations-related acts performed by Rwanda National Police and handed over to the Rwanda Investigation Bureau are of value as investigative acts.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: INVESTIGATION OF THE ORGAN IN CHARGE OF INVESTIGATION

blob
mark :537
original-oid 1c8d865b6e025892a7274eb7241eb0367a575a31
data 2005
Article 16: Responsibilities of an investigator
(1) In the performance of the investigation function, an investigator has the following responsibilities:
(a) to detect offences;
(b) to receive complaints and documents relating to offences;
(c) to gather incriminating and exculpatory evidence;
(d) to search for perpetrators of offences, co-offenders and their accomplices in order for them to be prosecuted by the public prosecution;
(e) to inform the victim and the suspect of the status of the file;
(f) to write a statement of arrest and detention and give a copy to the suspect;
(g) to release the suspect or release him or her by instructing him or her to comply with a set of conditions;
(h) to initiate mediation between the suspect and victim if –
(i) he or she believes that it is the appropriate way of providing remedy to the victim, reversing the consequences of the offence and helping the suspect mend his or her ways and come to his or her senses;
(ii) the offence committed is not punishable with imprisonment for a term exceeding 5 years;
(i) to close the file if –
(i) the constituent elements of an offence are incomplete;
(ii) the perpetrator of the offence has remained unknown;
(iii)there has been termination of criminal action;
(iv) or there is another reason provided for by the law.
(j) To inform the plaintiff of the decision to close his or her file within 30 days of such a decision being made. If not satisfied with such a decision, the plaintiff may refer the case to the public prosecution.
(2) Every month, files closed by an investigator are notified to the public prosecution.
(3) When conducting investigation, an investigator may carry out the arrest and detention of a suspect in accordance with procedures under this Law;
(4) The initiation of the mediation referred to in Paragraph (1)(h) of this Article cannot take place in the case of physical and sexual violence between spouses.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: INVESTIGATION OF THE ORGAN IN CHARGE OF INVESTIGATION

blob
mark :538
original-oid dc46f45ec70d5f184094b37d9a2c972a4adeac6e
data 821
Article 17: Powers to conduct preliminary investigations
(1) Preliminary investigations are conducted by investigators, on own motion, on complaint or on instructions from the public prosecution.
(2) However –
(a) if the organ in charge of investigations is seized of and rejects a complaint or refuses to conduct investigations without any justification, the claimant may directly file the complaint with the public prosecution. In that case, the public prosecution may conduct investigations after verifying whether the organ in charge of investigation was actually seized and failed to act;
(b) The public prosecution may also conduct investigations in case it believes there are those who had to be investigated but were not investigated.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: INVESTIGATION OF THE ORGAN IN CHARGE OF INVESTIGATION

blob
mark :539
original-oid ab6e04b8b1a7fb3a84ad1cdc87fe123da9ea4086
data 2389
Article 24: Responsibilities of a prosecutor upon receipt of the case file
(1) A prosecutor who receives a case file, has the following responsibilities:
(a) to immediately file the case file of the accused person with the competent court, if he or she believes that the case file is complete;
(b) to decide to conduct additional investigations if he or she believes that the evidence in the case file is insufficient;
(c) to initiate mediation between the suspect and victim if –
(i) he or she believes that it is the appropriate way of providing a remedy to the victim, reversing the consequences of the offence and helping the suspect mend his or her ways and come to his or her senses;
(ii) the offence committed is not punishable with imprisonment for a term exceeding 5 years;
(d) to impose a fine without trial;
(e) to initiate plea bargaining;
(f) to close the file if –
(i) the constituent elements of an offence are incomplete;
(ii) the perpetrator of the offence is unknown;
(iii)there is termination of criminal action;
(iv) he or she believes that prosecution of the suspect is unnecessary;
(v) or there is another reason provided for by the law;
(g) to release the suspect or release him or her by instructing him or her to comply with a set of conditions;
(h) to inform the victim and the suspect of the status of the file.
(2) If the imposition of a fine without trial referred to in Paragraph (1)(d) of this Article takes place when there is a victim of the offence who may file for compensation, the prosecutor summons the victim to facilitate negotiation between the victim and the suspect about the amount of compensation to be given. In case of disagreement, the victim files a claim for compensation with the competent court.
(3) The closure of a file referred to in Paragraph (1)(f) is an administrative decision that cannot preclude the continuation of investigations, in case the public prosecution finds any other incriminating evidence, provided the period of prescription of the criminal action has yet to expire. The victim of the offence and the suspect are informed of the decision within 30 days after the day on which such a decision is taken.
(4) The initiation of the mediation referred to in Paragraph (1)(c) cannot take place in the case of physical and sexual violence between spouses.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: INVESTIGATION OF THE PUBLIC PROSECUTION

blob
mark :540
original-oid 23cae0a783f90945405540aab13e0c8c132c9032
data 462
Article 49: Minor's testimony
(1) A minor who is at least 14 years old is qualified to testify as an adult.
(2) A minor aged under 14 years gives testimony without taking an oath. However, a court ruling cannot solely rely on his or her testimony. In that case, the testimony of a minor is supported by other corroborating evidence.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: COMMON PROVISIONS ON INVESTIGATION BETWEEN THE ORGAN IN CHARGE OF INVESTIGATION AND THE PUBLIC PROSECUTION

blob
mark :541
original-oid 28c68d92b237a716376361b07f0a8c50da222bc9
data 1615
Article 58: Management of a seized object
(1) An investigator or prosecutor who seizes an offence-related object must keep it with the greatest care. Any seized object is subject to a statement in which the identification of the object is described and is photographed.
(2) When a seized object is perishable, likely to depreciate or pose a serious risk to people's health, safety or property, the investigator or the prosecutor, on own initiative or upon request by any interested person, may immediately take any necessary measure such as returning it to the owner, placing it in the custody of its owner or other person, placing it under suretyship or destroying it. The investigator or the prosecutor makes a statement thereof, a copy of which is given to the owner of the object.
(3) In case a decision to return the seized object to the owner is taken but the owner is unavailable or one year has elapsed without him or her taking back the object, the organ in charge of investigation or the public prosecution may, in case it believes that the object may perish, expire or depreciate, auction it and the proceeds thereof are kept in the State Treasury until the owner appears. If the owner is available, the expenses incurred from the decision to return the object to the owner to the auction of the object are deducted from the proceeds given to the owner.
(4) An Order of the Minister in charge of justice determines modalities for the management and auction of seized object.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: COMMON PROVISIONS ON INVESTIGATION BETWEEN THE ORGAN IN CHARGE OF INVESTIGATION AND THE PUBLIC PROSECUTION

blob
mark :542
original-oid 0eadc3368b57215572d853ae33b5d5b59a21ce9b
data 344
Article 70: Electronic surveillance
(1) A suspect or an accused person may be subject to electronic surveillance.
(2) An Order of the Minister in charge of justice determines modalities for electronic surveillance.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: COMMON PROVISIONS ON INVESTIGATION BETWEEN THE ORGAN IN CHARGE OF INVESTIGATION AND THE PUBLIC PROSECUTION

blob
mark :543
original-oid abc8dff00b5ca4c4382d590c2c1ddd7b4cebd5bc
data 1764
Article 72: Statement
(1) The investigator or prosecutor, while preparing the statement of the offence committed, mainly focuses on the following:
(a) the identity of the person interrogated;
(b) the nature of the offence;
(c) the time of commission of the offence;
(d) the place of commission of the offence;
(e) circumstances of the offence;
(f) evidence incriminating or exculpating the suspect;
(g) statements of the persons who were present during the commission of the offence or who have clarifications to make thereon.
(2) If it is an interrogation statement, the investigator or prosecutor asks the person interrogated to read the statement and verify whether what is written corresponds to what he or she has said and signs or places his or her fingerprint on it if he or she is satisfied with its content. If the person interrogated does not know how to read, the investigator or prosecutor reads out the statement to him or her.
(3) If he or she refuses or is unable to sign or to place his or her fingerprint on the statement, such a fact is recorded in the statement.
(4) Errors identified in the statement are corrected by making an additional statement.
(5) A statement is concluded by the following written oath of the investigator or the prosecutor: "I hereby swear that this statement contains nothing but the truth". The statement also indicates the time and place of writing thereof, name and signature of the person having conducted interrogation.
(6) The organ in charge of investigation and the public prosecution determine the format and content of the statement and other documents used during investigation.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: COMMON PROVISIONS ON INVESTIGATION BETWEEN THE ORGAN IN CHARGE OF INVESTIGATION AND THE PUBLIC PROSECUTION

blob
mark :544
original-oid 515811f0417df14e0e23b109d2536ed95c3d34d8
data 1353
Article 76: Responsibilities of a judge during hearing on provisional detention
(1) The responsibilities of a judge who hears a provisional detention case include the following:
(a) to verify whether he or she is competent to hear such a provisional detention case brought before him or her;
(b) to verify whether the period of detention and other rights of the suspect have been respected during investigations;
(c) to examine whether there is no prescription or termination of the criminal action which would result in the issuance of provisional detention warrant being precluded;
(d) to examine whether there are serious grounds for provisional detention of the suspect;
(e) to examine whether there was an agreement of plea bargaining between the prosecutor and the accused person in order to take it into consideration while deciding on provisional detention;
(f) to take into consideration the accused person's living and health conditions;
(g) to analyse other arguments contained in the submissions of the parties.
(2) When the judge finds that constituent acts of the offence do not correspond to the classification assigned to the offence, he or she reclassifies the offence and orders detention or release of the suspect.
Chapter: CHAPTER IV: DETENTION AND PROVISIONAL RELEASE BY A JUDGE
Section: Section 2: Hearing on provisional detention

blob
mark :545
original-oid 06637dd5f6ac4eaa3ad0bee4be42b51da98af830
data 401
Article 169 bis: Trial of an extradited person sentenced by a Gacaca Court
A person extradited to Rwanda for trial and who was sentenced in absentia by a Gacaca Court is tried by the competent court. In this case, the court nullifies the decision rendered against him or her by the Gacaca Court.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: SPECIAL PROCEEDINGS
Section: Section 4: Prosecution and trial of a fugitive suspect

blob
mark :546
original-oid c12e9258f20b4ec9b86c178b40a7ae4746be6704
data 883
Article 173: Opposition of a case decided in absentia
(1) Opposition seeks to review a case decided in absentia and is instituted following the same procedure as that applying to the filing of a complaint.
(2) Opposition is instituted by the convict before the court having decided the case. However, opposition to a judgement rendered by a Gacaca Court is filed with the court competent to hear offences which were within the jurisdiction of the Gacaca Court having rendered the judgement, within the jurisdiction of which was situated that Gacaca Court.
(3) The submissions in support of opposition must indicate serious reasons having prevented the opposer from appearing at the hearing of the case subjected to opposition.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection One: Opposition of a case decided in absentia

blob
mark :547
original-oid c104c0f697ef76aae74bd5292d6d4274cde30ddb
data 776
Article 174: Time limits for filing opposition
(1) Opposition is filed within 15 days of the day on which the filing party is informed of the judgment.
(2) If there is no evidence that the person tried in absentia was served with the judgement, he or she may file opposition at any time before prescription of the penalty or before the execution of the judgement in relation to civil claims.
(3) For a judgement rendered by a Gacaca Court while the convict is abroad, and it is evident upon return that he or she did not evade justice, opposition is filed within 30 days of the date of his or her return to the country.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section One: Ordinary appeal procedure
Subsection: Subsection One: Opposition of a case decided in absentia

blob
mark :548
original-oid c1a450d699279d46264aa6c4a1353c7d58a82246
data 957
Article 199: Filing an action for case review
(1) The application for case review is filed following the same procedure as that applying to other actions.
(2) The application for case review of a judgement which has become final is not admissible in case it relates to a case for which review was applied on basis of the same evidence or same cause of action.
(3) In the event of an application for review in a case involving several accused persons, the court only hears the applicant for review. However, the accused persons other than the applicant for review may also be summoned to and speak at the hearing solely for the purpose of providing any clarifications in the case. The decision rendered can entail any modification concerning those other accused persons only when it may have an effect favourable to them.
Chapter: CHAPTER III: APPEAL PROCEDURE
Section: Section 2: Special appeal procedures
Subsection: Subsection One: Application for review

blob
mark :549
original-oid 321e236f69ab2006e4fddad855b6c6350e0ba36d
data 1070
Article 216: Organs and persons responsible for the execution of judgements
Organs and persons responsible for the execution of judgements are the following:
(a) Rwanda Correctional Service, National Public Prosecution Authority, Rwanda Investigation Bureau, Rwanda National Police, Military Prosecution Department and the organ in charge of military correctional facility, in case of imprisonment penalty;
(b) Rwanda Correctional Service, local Government officers empowered to do so by the Minister in charge of justice, and the organ in charge of military correctional facility, in case of sentence of community service;
(c) National Public Prosecution Authority, Rwanda Investigation Bureau, Rwanda National Police and Military Prosecution Department, in case of additional penalty;
(d) the court bailiff, in case of a fine and damages;
(e) the court registrar with respect to the collection of fines and court fees, in case of judicial attachment of an object of a convict.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: EXECUTION OF COURT DECISION
Section: Section One: General provisions

blob
mark :550
original-oid 5073dcb9136830665b0ac57ab5eeb6ddf563027d
data 876
Article 233: Period of imprisonment to be completed before applying for release on parole
The applicant for release on parole may be granted such release based on the term of imprisonment he or she is sentenced to and the term of imprisonment he or she has served, as follows:
(a) if he or she was sentenced to imprisonment for a term not exceeding 5 years and has served at least one fourth of the penalty;
(b) if he or she was sentenced to imprisonment for a term exceeding 5 years and has served at least one third of the penalty;
(c) if he or she was sentenced to life imprisonment and has served at least 15 years of imprisonment.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: GROUNDS FOR SUSPENSION OF THE EXECUTION OF A PENALTY AND REMOVAL OF A PENALTY
Section: Section One: Grounds for suspension of the execution of penalty
Subsection: Subsection 2: Release on parole of the convicted person

blob
mark :551
original-oid 903206dc9a90960f31d5292adebba958bad8ad3b
data 1537
Article 234: Application procedure for release on parole
(1) An application for release on parole is filed with the Minister in charge of justice through the Commissioner General of Rwanda Correctional Service. However, an applicant for release on parole who is detained in a military correctional facility applies to the Minister in charge of justice for release on parole through the Minister in charge of defence.
(2) At least once a year, the Minister in charge of defence and the Commissioner General of Rwanda Correctional Service submit to the Minister in charge of justice a list of applicants for release on parole.
(3) The list of applicants for release on parole indicates the following:
(a) the identification of the applicant for release on parole;
(b) the offence for which the applicant is incarcerated;
(c) the decision of the court having sentenced the applicant at final instance;
(d) the sentence served and the remainder of the sentence;
(e) the applicant's conduct in a correctional facility with supporting evidence;
(f) any other necessary information.
(4) An Order of the Minister in charge of justice approves the release on parole of a person sentenced to imprisonment and conditions imposed on him or her.
(5) The decision on release on parole is not subject to appeal.
Chapter: CHAPTER II: GROUNDS FOR SUSPENSION OF THE EXECUTION OF A PENALTY AND REMOVAL OF A PENALTY
Section: Section One: Grounds for suspension of the execution of penalty
Subsection: Subsection 2: Release on parole of the convicted person

blob
mark :552
original-oid b26df369eeb936cfbfb5312484e64ef0112c97ac
data 1255
Article 264 bis: Reconstitution of a copy of Gacaca Court judgement
(1) A person who needs a copy of a judgment rendered by a Gacaca Court but which cannot be found, requests the Public Prosecution at the primary level to recollect information for the reconstitution of the judgement. The Public Prosecution submits, in the form of application, the information collected to the Primary Court in order for it to reconstitute the judgement.
(2) An application for reconstitution of a copy of judgement rendered by a Gacaca Court is adjudicated by way of a unilateral application. A decision thereon is rendered within 30 days from the date of adjudication of the application.
(3) If a copy of judgement for a person sentenced to imprisonment by a Gacaca Court is not found while he or she has not served his or her sentence or has served a part thereof, and there is evidence that the person was sentenced, he or she may be placed under arrest and provisional detention and brought before the court with which an application for reconstitution of a judgement is filed. The court may order his or her provisional detention for a period not exceeding 30 days pending the reconstitution of the copy of judgement.
Chapter: CHAPTER ONE: MISCELLANEOUS PROVISIONS

